{"file_size":817703,"filename":"I3344-2021-2022-Part-6 (5).pdf","level":"info","message":"Input object for PDFDocument:","mime_type":"application/pdf","original_text":"\n\nI3344\nNumerical Simulation & \nModeling\nAhmad Fadlallah\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nTentative Syllabus\n✓Interpolation and extrapolation\n✓Linear: linear regression y = ax + b; correlation, standard deviation, etc. \n✓Non-linear: k nearest neighbors (KNN)\n✓Validation of two models: k-fold cross validation method.\n✓Solving a linear equation\n✓Direct methods: Gauss and LU\n✓Iterative methods: Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel\n✓Derivation\n✓Finite difference method (FDM): Euler and Runge-kutta\n✓Integration: surface estimation\n✓Finite Element Method (FEM). \n✓Monte Carlo method. \n✓Comparison of two methods.\n✓Non-linear problems\n✓Bisection method\n•Optimization\n•Introduction to the notion of parallel computing and underlying algorithms\n2\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nOutline\n•Introduction\n•Golden Section Search\n•Newton Method\n3\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction\n•Root locationand optimizationare relatedin the sense \nthat both involve guessingand searchingfor a pointon a \nfunction.\n๏Root location: searching for zeros of a function\n๏Optimization: searching for the minimum or the maximum.\n4\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nMathematical Background\n•An optimization or mathematical programming problem \ngenerally be stated as:\nFind x, which minimizesor maximizesf(x) subject to\n•x is an n-dimensional design vector\n•f(x) is the objective function\n•d\ni\n(x) are inequality constraints\n•e\ni\n(x) are equality constraints\n•a\ni\nand b\ni\nare constants\n5\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n6\nOne-\nDimensional \nUnconstraine\nd \nOptimization\nMultidimensio\nnal \nUnconstraine\nd \nOptimization\nConstrained \nOptimization\nOptimization\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n•Multimodalcases: Both localand globaloptimacan \noccur for a single function \n7\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n•How to distinguish global optimum from local one?\n๏By graphingto gain insight into the behavior of the \nfunction.\n๏Using randomly generated starting guessesand picking \nthe largest of the optimaas global.\n๏Perturbing the starting point associated to a local \noptima to see if the routine returns a better point or the \nsame local optima.\n8\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search\n•A unimodalfunction has a singlemaximum (resp. \nminimum) in the a given interval. For a unimodal \nfunction:\n๏Pick two pointsthat will bracket your extremum [x\nl\n, x\nu\n].\n๏Pick an additional third pointwithin this intervalto \ndetermine whether a maximum occurred.\n๏Pick a fourth pointto determine whether the maximum \nhas occurred within the first three or last three points\n๏The key is making this approach efficient by choosing \nintermediate points wiselythus minimizing the function \nevaluationsby replacing the old values with new values.\n9\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•The sum of the two sub lengths 푙\n1\nand 푙\n2\nmust equal the original interval length.\n•The ratio of the length must be equal\n10\nGolden Ratio\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden Ratio (History)\n11\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•The method starts with two initial \nguesses, 푥\n푙\nand 푥\n푢\n, that bracket \none local extremum of f(x)\n•two interior points 푥\n1\nand 푥\n2\nare \nchosen according to the golden \nratio\n•The function is evaluated at these \ntwo interior points.\n12\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•If f(x\n1\n)>f(x\n2\n) \n=> the domain of x to the left of x\n2\nfrom x\nl\nto x\n2 \ndoes not contain the maximum \n=> can be eliminated => new x\nl\n= x\n2\n•If f(x\n2\n)>f(x\n1\n)\n=> the domain of x to the right of x\n1\nfrom x\nl\nto x\n2\n, \nwould have been eliminated => new x\nu\n= x\n1\n•New x\n1\nis determined as before\n13\n•Stop condition: |x\nu\n-x\nl\n| < Ɛ, max = (x\nl\n+ x\nu\n)/2\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use the golden-section search to find the maximum of the \nfollowing function  within the interval x\nl\n=0 and x\nu\n=4.\n•Note\n14\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n15\n•Because f(x\n2\n) > f(x\n1\n), the maximum is in the interval defined by x\nl\n, x\n2\n, and x\n1\n. \n•For the new interval\n•LB remains x\nl\n= 0, x\n1\nbecomes the UB => x\nu\n= 2.472. \n•Former x\n2\nvalue becomes the new x\n1\n, that is, x\n1\n= 1.528.\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution (cont’d)\n16\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nQuadratic Interpolation\n•Parabolic interpolation takes advantage of the fact that a \nsecond-order polynomialoften provides a good \napproximationto the shape of f(x) near an optimum\n17\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nQuadratic Interpolation (cont’d)\n•There is only onequadratic polynomial or parabola\nconnecting three points. \n•if we have three points that jointly bracket an optimum, we \ncan fit a parabola to the points. \n•Then we can differentiate it, set the result equal to zero, and \nsolve for an estimate of the optimal x. \n•It can be shown through some algebraic manipulations that the \nresult is\n•x\n0\n, x\n1\n, and x\n2\nare the initial guesses, and x\n3\nis the value of xthat \ncorresponds to the maximum value of the parabolic fitto the \nguesses\n•and so on (same as golden section search)\n18\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use parabolic interpolation to approximate the maximum \nof the following function  with initial guesses of x\n0\n=0, x\n1=\n1, \nand x\n2 = \n4.\n•Note:\n19\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n20\nBecause the function value for the new point is higher than for the intermediate \npoint (x\n1\n) and the new x value is to the right of the intermediate point, the lower \nguess (x\n0\n) is discarded. Therefore, for the next iteration\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n21\nGolden Section Search\nQuadratic\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nNewton’s Method\n•Based on the Newton-Raphson \nmethod to find the root of an \nequation\n•The Newton-Raphson method is \nbased on the principle that if the \ninitial guess of the root of f(x) = \n0 is at x\ni\n, then if one draws the \ntangent to the curve at f(x\ni\n),the \npoint x\ni+1\nwhere the tangent \ncrosses the x-axis is an \nimproved estimate of the root \n22\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nNewton’s Method (cont’d)\n•Consider a new function g(x)=f'(x). \n•The optimal value x* satisfies: f'(x*)=g(x*)=0\n•We can use the following as a technique to the extremum \nof f(x).\n•Start with an initial guess x\n0\nand repeat the iterations until \nfinding f’(x\nk\n) = 0 (or less than some value)\n23\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use Newton’s method to approximate the maximum of the \nfollowing function  with initial guesses of x\n0\n=2.5\n•Note:\n24\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n25\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\nEmploy the following methods to find the maximum of\nf (x) = 4x-1.8x\n2\n+ 1.2x\n3\n-0.3x\n4\n(a) Golden-section search (x\nl\n= -2, x\nu\n= 4, Ɛ\ns\n= 1%).\n(b) Parabolic interpolation (x\n0\n=1.75, x\n1\n=2, x\n2\n=2.5, iter. = 4).\n(c)  Newton’s method (x\n0\n= 3, Ɛ\ns\n=1%).\n26","storage_path":"uploads\\1774207091854_I3344_2021_2022_Part_6__5_.pdf","summary":"Here's a detailed summary of the provided text, acting as an expert study assistant:\n\n**I3344 Numerical Simulation & Modeling**\n**Instructor:** Ahmad Fadlallah\n\n**I. Course Overview (Tentative Syllabus)**\n*   **Interpolation and Extrapolation:**\n    *   **Linear:** Linear regression (`y = ax + b`), correlation, standard deviation.\n    *   **Non-linear:** K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN).\n*   **Validation of Models:** K-fold cross-validation method.\n*   **Solving Linear Equations:**\n    *   **Direct Methods:** Gauss elimination, LU decomposition.\n    *   **Iterative Methods:** Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel.\n*   **Derivation:**\n    *   Finite Difference Method (FDM): Euler, Runge-Kutta.\n*   **Integration:** Surface estimation.\n*   **Finite Element Method (FEM).**\n*   **Monte Carlo Method.**\n*   **Comparison of two methods.**\n*   **Non-linear Problems:** Bisection method.\n*   **Optimization.**\n*   **Introduction to Parallel Computing:** Notion and underlying algorithms.\n\n**II. Presentation Specific Topics (Outline)**\n*   Introduction to Optimization\n*   Golden Section Search\n*   Newton Method\n*   *Note: Quadratic Interpolation is also covered within the presentation.*\n\n**III. Introduction to Optimization**\n*   **Relationship between Root Location and Optimization:**\n    *   Both involve guessing and searching for a point on a function.\n    *   **Root location:** Searching for zeros of a function (`f(x) = 0`).\n    *   **Optimization:** Searching for the minimum or maximum of a function.\n*   **Mathematical Background (Optimization Problem Definition):**\n    *   General statement: Find `x` that minimizes or maximizes `f(x)` subject to constraints.\n    *   `x`: An n-dimensional design vector.\n    *   `f(x)`: The objective function (the function to be optimized).\n    *   `d_i(x)`: Inequality constraints.\n    *   `e_i(x)`: Equality constraints.\n    *   `a_i` and `b_i`: Constants.\n*   **Types of Optimization:**\n    *   One-Dimensional Unconstrained Optimization.\n    *   Multidimensional Unconstrained Optimization.\n    *   Constrained Optimization.\n*   **Multimodal Cases:**\n    *   A single function can have both local and global optima (multiple peaks/valleys).\n    *   **Strategies to Distinguish Global Optimum from Local Optima:**\n        *   Graphing the function to gain visual insight.\n        *   Using multiple randomly generated starting guesses and selecting the best found optimum.\n        *   Perturbing the starting point associated with a local optimum to see if the routine finds a better point or returns to the same local optimum.\n\n**IV. Golden-Section Search**\n*   **Purpose:** An efficient method for finding the extremum (maximum or minimum) of a unimodal function within a given interval.\n*   **Key Concept: Unimodal Function:** A function that has a single maximum (or minimum) within a specified interval.\n*   **Core Idea:** Iteratively narrows the search interval by evaluating the function at two internal points chosen according to the golden ratio, minimizing the number of function evaluations.\n*   **Method Steps (for finding a maximum):**\n    1.  **Bracketing:** Start with two initial guesses, `x_l` (lower bound) and `x_u` (upper bound), that bracket the extremum.\n    2.  **Interior Points:** Choose two interior points `x_1` and `x_2` within `[x_l, x_u]` such that the ratio of lengths `(x_u - x_1)` to `(x_1 - x_l)` (and similarly for `x_2`) is equal to the **golden ratio** `(approximately 0.618)`.\n        *   This ensures that one of the interior points can be reused in the next iteration.\n    3.  **Evaluation:** Evaluate the objective function at `f(x_1)` and `f(x_2)`.\n    4.  **Interval Reduction (for maximum):**\n        *   If `f(x_1) > f(x_2)`: The maximum is in the interval `[x_l, x_2]`. The new `x_u` becomes `x_2`.\n        *   If `f(x_2) > f(x_1)`: The maximum is in the interval `[x_1, x_u]`. The new `x_l` becomes `x_1`.\n    5.  **Iteration:** A new `x_1` (or `x_2`) is determined, and the process repeats.\n    6.  **Stop Condition:** The process continues until the interval width `|x_u - x_l|` is less than a predefined tolerance `ε`.\n    7.  **Extremum Estimate:** The maximum (or minimum) is estimated as `(x_l + x_u) / 2`.\n\n**V. Quadratic Interpolation (Parabolic Interpolation)**\n*   **Purpose:** Approximate the optimum (maximum or minimum) of a function using a second-order polynomial (parabola).\n*   **Core Idea:** A parabola often provides a good approximation to the shape of `f(x)` near an optimum, and only three points are needed to define a unique parabola.\n*   **Method Steps:**\n    1.  **Initial Guesses:** Start with three points `x_0`, `x_1`, and `x_2` that jointly bracket an optimum.\n    2.  **Parabola Fit:** Fit a unique parabola through these three points `(x_0, f(x_0))`, `(x_1, f(x_1))`, and `(x_2, f(x_2))`.\n    3.  **Optimal `x` Estimate:** The optimum `x_3` of the fitted parabola is calculated using the following formula:\n        `x_3 = [f(x_0)(x_1^2 - x_2^2) + f(x_1)(x_2^2 - x_0^2) + f(x_2)(x_0^2 - x_1^2)] / [2 * f(x_0)(x_1 - x_2) + 2 * f(x_1)(x_2 - x_0) + 2 * f(x_2)(x_0 - x_1)]`\n    4.  **Iteration:** One of the initial points (`x_0`, `x_1`, or `x_2`) is discarded and replaced by `x_3` for the next iteration, similar to the Golden Section Search, to keep the optimum bracketed by the three points.\n\n**VI. Newton's Method for Optimization**\n*   **Purpose:** Locate the extremum (maximum or minimum) of a function `f(x)`.\n*   **Basis:** Adapted from the Newton-Raphson method, which is used to find the roots (`g(x) = 0`) of an equation.\n*   **Core Idea:** An optimal value `x*` satisfies `f'(x*) = 0` (the first derivative is zero at an extremum). Therefore, Newton's method is applied to find the root of the *first derivative* function, `g(x) = f'(x)`.\n*   **Newton-Raphson Formula (for finding roots of `g(x)=0`):**\n    `x_{i+1} = x_i - g(x_i) / g'(x_i)`\n*   **Newton's Method Formula (for finding extremum of `f(x)`):**\n    *   Since `g(x) = f'(x)`, then `g'(x) = f''(x)`.\n    *   Therefore, the iterative formula for optimization is:\n        `x_{k+1} = x_k - f'(x_k) / f''(x_k)`\n*   **Method Steps:**\n    1.  **Initial Guess:** Start with an initial guess `x_0`.\n    2.  **Iteration:** Repeatedly apply the formula `x_{k+1} = x_k - f'(x_k) / f''(x_k)`.\n    3.  **Stop Condition:** Continue iterations until `f'(x_k)` is approximately zero (or falls below a specified tolerance value).\n\n**VII. Comparative Exercise**\n*   The text concludes with an exercise demonstrating the application of Golden-section search, Parabolic interpolation, and Newton's method to find the maximum of a given function `f(x) = 4x - 1.8x^2 + 1.2x^3 - 0.3x^4` with specified initial conditions and stopping criteria for each method.","timestamp":"2026-03-22 21:18:32"}
