{"file_size":1990454,"filename":"I3344-2021-2022-Part-3.pdf","level":"info","message":"Input object for PDFDocument:","mime_type":"application/pdf","original_text":"\n\nI3344 \nNumerical Simulation & \nModeling\nAhmad Fadlallah\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nTentative Syllabus\n✓\nInterpolation and extrapolation\n\n✓\nLinear: linear regression y = ax + b; correlation, standard deviation, etc. \n\n✓\nNon-linear: k nearest neighbors (KNN)\n\n✓\nValidation of two models: k-fold cross validation method.\n\n✓\nSolving a linear equation\n\n✓\nDirect methods: Gauss and LU\n\n✓\nIterative methods: Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel\n\n•\nDerivation \n๏\nFinite difference method (FDM): Euler and Runge-kutta \n•\nIntegration: surface estimation\n\n๏Monte Carlo method. \n\n๏\nFinite Element Method (FEM). \n\n๏Comparison of two methods.\n\n•\nNon-linear problems\n\n๏Bisection method\n\n•\nIntroduction to the notion of parallel computing and underlying algorithms\n!2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nOutline\n•\nWhat differential equations are?\n\n•\nFirst order differential equations\n\n•\nSolving first order equations using Euler’s method. \n\n•\nAnalysis of Euler’s method \n\n•\nTaylor methods\n\n•\nEuler’s midpoint and Runge-Kutta Methods\n!3\n\nWhat are Differential \nEquations?\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nIntroduction\n•\nDifferential equations is an essential tool in a wide range \nof applications. \n\n•\nMany phenomena can be modeled by a relationship \nbetween a function and its derivatives\n!5\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExamples from Physics\n•\nConsider an object moving through space. \n\n•\nAt time t = 0: it is located at a point P \n\n•\nAfter a time t: its distance to P corresponds to f(t)\n\n•\nAverage speed \u0000: \u0000\n\n•\nSpeed at time t : \n\n•\nAverage acceleration \u0000:  ! \n•\nAcceleration at time t: \n[t,t+Δt]\nf(t+Δt)−f(t)\nΔt\n[t,t+Δt]\nv(t+Δt)−v(t)\nΔt\n!6\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExamples from Physics\n•\nConsider an object moving through space. \n\n•\nAt time t = 0: it is located at a point P \n\n•\nAfter a time t: its distance to P corresponds to f(t)\n\n•\nAverage speed \u0000: \u0000\n\n•\nSpeed at time t : \n\n•\nAverage acceleration \u0000:  ! \n•\nAcceleration at time t: \n[t,t+Δt]\nf(t+Δt)−f(t)\nΔt\n[t,t+Δt]\nv(t+Δt)−v(t)\nΔt\n!6\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExamples from Physics (cont’d)\n•\nNewton’s second law: if an object is influenced by a \nforce, its acceleration is proportional to the force\n\n! \n•\nExample: object of mass m falling freely towards the earth\n\n๏\n Influenced by two opposite forces: gravity and friction\n\n๏\nGravitational force: F\ng\n= mg\n\n๏\nFriction: proportional to : F\nf \n= cv\n2\n\n\n!\nF=ma\nv\n2\nF=F\ng\n−F\nf\n=ma⇒mg−cv(t)\n2\n=mv′\u0000(t)⇒mv′\u0000=mg−cv\n2\n!7\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeneral use of differential equations\n1.A quantity of interest is modeled by a function x\n\n2.A relation between x  and its derivatives is derived from \nsome known principle => differential equation is obtained\n\n3.The differential equation is solved by a mathematical or \nnumerical method.\n\n4.The solution of the equation is interpreted in the context of \nthe original problem.\n!8\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Different types of differential equations\n•\nFirst order differential equation\n\n๏\nInvolves the unknown function \u0000 and its first derivative \u0000\n\n•\nP\nth\n order differential equation\n\n๏\nInvolves higher derivatives up to order p\n\n•\nLinear differential equations\n\n! \n•\nOrdinary differential equations\n\n๏\nUnknown function depends on only one variable (denoted by t)\n\n•\nPartial differential equations\n\n๏\nUnknown function depends on two or more variables\n\n๏\nExample: three coordinates of a point in space\nxx′\u0000\nx\n(p)\n(t)=f(t)+g\n0\n(t)x(t)+g\n1\n(t)x′\u0000(t)+g\n2\n(t)x′\u0000′\u0000(t)+...+g\np\n(t)x\n(p−1)\n(t)\n!9\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercises\n•\nClassify the following differential equations:\n!10\nx′\u0000\nx\n•\nFirst order differential equation \n•\nPth order differential equation \n•\nLinear differential equations \n•\nOrdinary differential equations \n•\nPartial differential equations\n\nFirst Order Differential \nEquations\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nIntroduction\n•\nx’ = f (t ,x) \n•\nx = x(t) \n•\nt is the free variable\n\n•\nExamples of first order differential equations\n\n๏\n! \n๏\n! \n๏\n! \n๏\n! \n๏\n!\nx′\u0000=3\nx′\u0000=2t\nx′\u0000=x\nx′\u0000=t\n3\n+x\nx′\u0000=sin(tx)\n!12\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nScope\nDerive numerical methods for solving differential \nequations in the form ! where \u0000 is a given \nfunction of two variables\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nf\n!13\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Initial conditions\n•\nAnalytical solutions of differential equations involve a \ngeneral constant C (like indefinite integrals)\n\n•\nNeed to supply an extra condition that will specify the value \nof the constant (called initial condition)\n\n•\nStandard way:  specify one point on the solution of the \nequation => solution should satisfy x(a) = x\n0\n for certain a\n\n•\nExample: x’=2x => x = Ce\n2t \n ∀C \n \n๏\nInitial value: x(0) = 1 => C = 1=> x(t) = e\n2t \n๏General Initial value: x(a) = x\n0 \n=> x(t) = x\n0 \ne\n2(t-a) \n•\nInitial condition usually has a concrete physical interpretation\n\n๏\nExample: initial speed of a falling object\n!14\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nDefinition revisited\n“A  first  order  differential  equation is  an equation in the  form \n \nx'=f(t,  x),  where  f  (t,  x)  is  a  function  of  two  variables.  In  general, \nthis kind of equation has many solutions, but a specific solution is \nobtained by adding an initial condition x(a) = x\n0\n . \n \nA complete formulation of a first order differential equation is: \nx'= f (t, x), \n \nx(a) = x\n0 \n” \n!15\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeometric interpretation\n•\nAt any point (t, x), the equation \u0000 prescribes the \nslope of the solution through this point\n\n•\nExample: \u0000\n\n๏\nThis equation describes a family of functions whose \ntangents have slope \u0000 at any point \u0000\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)=t\nt(x,t)\n!16\nx(0)= i/10 for i= 0, 1, . . . , 10\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeometric interpretation\n•\nAt any point (t, x), the equation \u0000 prescribes the \nslope of the solution through this point\n\n•\nExample: \u0000\n\n๏\nThis equation describes a family of functions whose \ntangents have slope \u0000 at any point \u0000\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)=t\nt(x,t)\n!16\nx(0)= i/10 for i= 0, 1, . . . , 10\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeometric interpretation\n•\nAt any point (t, x), the equation \u0000 prescribes the \nslope of the solution through this point\n\n•\nExample: \u0000\n\n๏\nThis equation describes a family of functions whose \ntangents have slope \u0000 at any point \u0000\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)=t\nt(x,t)\n!16\nx(0)= i/10 for i= 0, 1, . . . , 10\nSlope field\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Conditions of existence of one solution\n•\nExample: \n๏\nCondition: -1 <= x <= +1\n\n•\nMain condition: \u0000 should no have any problematic \nbehavior\n\n•\nExistence and uniqueness\nf(t,x)\n!17\nx′\u0000=1−x\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Conditions of existence of one solution\n•\nExercise: What  features  of  the  following  differential  equations \ncould cause problems if you try to solve them?\n!18\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeneral strategy for numerical solution of \ndifferential equations\n•\nSuppose the differential equation and initial condition: \n, \n \n \n \n  \nare given together, with an interval \n! where a solution is sought. \n\n•\nObjective:\n\n๏\nAnalytical Solutions: Find \u0000\n\n๏\nNumerical Solutions: Approximate \u0000\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)x(a)=x\n0\n[a,b]\nx(t)\nx(t)\n!19\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeneral strategy for numerical solution of \ndifferential equations (cont’d)\n•\n, \n \n \n \n  \n\n\n•\nSuppose also that an increasing sequence of t-values \n \nare given, with  and  , which in the \nfollowing will be equally spaced with step length ! , i.e., \n! ,   for ! .\n\n•\nA numerical method for solving the equation is a recipe \nfor computing a sequence of numbers ! such that \n! is an approximation to the true solution ! at !   \n\n•\nFor k > 0, the approximation ! is computed  from one or \nmore of the previous approximations !. \n\n•\nContinuous approximation is obtained by connecting \nneighboring points by straight lines.\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)x(a)=x\n0\n(t\nk\n)\nk=0...n\na=t\n0\nb=t\nn\nh\nt\nk\n=a+k.hk=0,...,n\nx\n0\n,x\n1\n,...,x\nn\nx\nk\nx(t\nk\n)\nt\nk\nx\nk\nx\nk−1\n,x\nk−2\n,...,x\n0\n!20\n\nEuler’s method\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nIntroduction\n•\nMethods for finding analytical solutions of differential \nequations often appear rather tricky and unintuitive. \n\n•\nMany numerical methods are based on simple, often \ngeometric ideas.\n\n•\nEuler’s Method\n\n๏\nThe simplest of numerical methods\n\n๏\nBased directly on geometric interpretation of first order \ndifferential equation\n!22\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Basic idea and algorithm\n•\n! \n•\nAim: to compute a sequence of approximations \n! to the solution where ! \n\n•\nInitial condition provides a point on the true solution ! \n๏\n\u0000 is the natural starting poi