ntiation/Derivation\nExercise\n•\nConsider the differential equation: x'= t\n3\n-2x, x(0) = 0.25.                               \n\n•\nSuppose we want  to compute an approximation to the \nsolution at the points t\n1\n = 0.1, t\n2\n = 0.2, ..., \n \nt\n10\n = 1, i.e., the  \npoints  tk = k*h for k = 1, 2, ... , 10, with h = 0.1\n!27\nReminder:  \nIn  Euler’s  method,  an  approximate  solution  (t\nk\n  ,x\nk\n  )  is  advanced  to \n(t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n) by following the tangent: \nT\nk\n(t) = x\nk\n + (t -t\nk\n)x’\nk\n = x\nk\n + (t - t\nk\n ) f(t\nk\n ,x\nk\n ) at (t\nk\n, x\nk\n) from t\nk\n to t\nk+ 1\n= t\nk\n +h.   \nThis results in the approximation: x\nk+1\n = x\nk\n + hf(t\nk\n, x\nk\n) at x(t\nk+1\n)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercise - Solution\n•\nx'= t\n3\n-2x\n!28\nit\ni\nx\ni\nx'\n0\nT\ni\n(t)\n000,25-0,50,25-0,5t\n10,10,2-0,3990,2 − (t − 0,1)0,399\n20,20,1601\n30,3 0,1289\n40,4 0,1058\n50,5 0,0910\n60,6 0,0853\n70,7 0,0899\n80,8 0,1062\n90,9 0,1362\n101 0,1818\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nAlgorithm\n•\nLet the differential equation \u0000 be given together with \nthe initial condition \u0000, the solution interval [a, b], and \nthe number of steps \u0000 . \n\n•\nIf the following algorithm is performed\n\nh = (b − a )/n ; \nt\n0\n = a ; \nfor k = 0, 1, . . . , n − 1 \nx\nk +1\n = x\nk\n \n \n+ h f (t\nk\n , x\nk\n ); \nt\nk +1\n = a + (k + 1)h ; \n•\nthe value \u0000 will be an approximation to the solution \u0000 of \nthe differential equation, for each \u0000 \nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nx(a)=x\n0\nn\nx\nk\nx(t\nk\n)\nk=0,1,...,n\n!29\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nGeometric Interpretation\n•\nEuler's method may be interpreted as stepping between different solution curves of \nthe equation x'= f(t,x).\n\n๏\nThe initial condition is updated at each step\n\n•\nAt time t, the tangent T\nk\n to the solution curve given by\n\nx’= f(t, x) , x(t\nk\n) = x\nk\n \nis  followed to the  point (t\nk+1\n, x\nk+ 1\n), which is a point on the solution  curve given by\n\nx’= f(t, x) , x(t\nk+1\n) = x\nk+1\n!30\nx' = cos6t/(l + t+ x\n2\n ) , x\n0\n = 0\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercises\nUse  Euler’s  method  with three  steps  with  h  =  0.1  to  compute \napproximate solutions of the following differential equations:\n!31\n\nError analysis for Euler’s \nmethod\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nBackground\n•\nA  function g(t,  x)  of  two  variables  can  be differentiated  with \nrespect  to \u0000  (resp. \u0000)  by  considering \u0000  (resp. \u0000)  to  be  a \nconstant; the resulting derivative is denoted g'\nt\n (t, x) (resp. g'\nx\n \n(t, x))  \n\n•\nTaylor polynomial Approximation: Any function  \u0000 whose \nfirst \u0000 derivatives are continuous at \u0000 can be \nexpanded in a Taylor polynomial  of degree \u0000  at \u0000  with \na corresponding error term,\n\nwhere \u0000 is a number in the interval \u0000 (resp. \u0000 if \u0000 ) \nthat depends on x. \n\nThis is called a Taylor expansion of the function \u0000\ntxxt\nf\nn+1x=a\nnx=a\nξ\nx\n[a,x][x,a]x<a\nf\n!33\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nBackground\n•\nMean value theorem: Let ! be a function of the two \nvariables ! and !, and let ! denote the derivative of ! with \nrespect to . If \n \nis continuous in  then\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ! \nwhere 휉 is a number in the interval (x\n1\n,x\n2\n) \ng(t,x)\ntxg′\u0000\nx\ng\nxg′\u0000\nx\n[x\n1\n,x\n2\n]\ng(t,x\n2\n)−g(t,x\n1\n)=g′\u0000\nx\n(t,ξ)(x\n2\n−x\n1\n)\n!34\n•\nExercise: Apply the \nmean value theorem for \nthe following function \ng(t, x ) = t x + t\n2\nx\n2\n \n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nBackground\n•\nMean value theorem: Let ! be a function of the two \nvariables ! and !, and let ! denote the derivative of ! with \nrespect to . If \n \nis continuous in  then\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ! \nwhere 휉 is a number in the interval (x\n1\n,x\n2\n) \ng(t,x)\ntxg′\u0000\nx\ng\nxg′\u0000\nx\n[x\n1\n,x\n2\n]\ng(t,x\n2\n)−g(t,x\n1\n)=g′\u0000\nx\n(t,ξ)(x\n2\n−x\n1\n)\n!34\n•\nExercise: Apply the \nmean value theorem for \nthe following function \ng(t, x ) = t x + t\n2\nx\n2\n \n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and global error\n•\nEven though the local error at each step may be quite \nsmall, the total (global) error may accumulate and \nbecome much bigger.\n!35\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and Global Errors\n•\nBasic idea in Euler’s method: x\nk+1\n = x\nk\n + hf(t\nk\n, x\nk\n) at x(t\nk+1\n) \n•\nApproximation: x(t\nk+1\n) ≈ x\nk+1\n = x\nk\n + hf(t\nk\n, x\nk\n) \n•\nSimple Taylor Approximation\n\n휉\nk \n∈ [t\nk\n, t\nk+1\n] \n!36\n•\nGlobal error accumulated:\nlocal error\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and Global Errors (cont’d)\n!37\n•\nMean Value Theorem\nθ\nk \nis a number in the interval  (x\nk\n \n, x (t\nk\n))\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and Global Errors (cont’d)\n•\nIf the two first derivatives of f  exist, the error in using Euler’s method for \nsolving x'= f(t, x) develops according to the relation\n\n•\nwhere 휉\nk\n  is a number in the interval (t\nk\n , t\nk +1\n) and θ\nk\n  is a number in the \ninterval  x\nk\n, x(t\nk\n). \n\n•\nThe global error at step k + 1 has two sources:\n\n๏\nThe advancement of the global error at step k to the next step\n\n๏\nThe local truncation error committed by only including two terms in the \nTaylor polynomial\n!38\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and Global Errors (cont’d)\n!39\nUnstable Solution\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLocal and Global Errors (cont’d)\n!40\nStable Solution\n\nTaylor Methods\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nDifferentiating the differential equations\n•\nHow to determine higher order derivatives of the \nsolution of a differential equation at a point \n๏\nExample: ! . \n✴\n! \n✴\n! \n✴\n! \n✴\nHigher order derivatives can be determined similarly \nx′\u0000(t)=t+x\n2\n(t),x(a)=x\n0\nAtx=a:x(a)=x\n0\n,x′\u0000(a)=a+x\n2\n0\n.\nx′\u0000′\u0000(t)=1+2.x(t).x′\u0000(t)=1+2.x(t).(t+x\n2\n(t))\nx′\u0000′\u0000(a)=1+2.x(a).x′\u0000(a)=1+2x\n0\n.(a+x\n2\n0\n)\n!42\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nDifferentiating the differential equations\n•\nLet ! be a differential equation with initial \ncondition ! \n•\nSuppose that the derivatives of ! of order ! exist \nat the point !. \n\n•\nThen the ! derivative of the solution ! at ! can be \nexpressed in terms of \u0000 and !: \n\n!, \n\nwhere \u0000 is a function defined by \u0000 and its derivatives of \norder less than \u0000\nx′\u0000(t)=f(t,x)\nx(a)=x\n0\nf(t,x)\np−1\n(a,x\n0\n)\np\nth\nx(t)\nx=a\na\nx\n0\nx\n(p)\n(a)=F\np\n(a,x\n0\n)\nF\np\nf\np\n!43\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercises\n•\nCompute ! and ! of the following differential equations \nat the given initial value.\nx′\u0000′\u0000(a)x′\u0000′\u0000′\u0000(a)\n!44\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nIntroduction\n•\nMain idea: to approximate the solution by a Taylor \npolynomial of a suitable degree.\n\n•\nEuler’s method is the simplest Taylor method\n\n! \n•\nQuadratic Taylor method approximation\n\n!\nx(t+h)≈x(t)+hx′\u0000(t)\nx(t+h)≈x(t)+hx′\u0000(t)+\nh\n2\n2\nx′\u0000′\u0000(t)\n!45\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nQuadratic Taylor Method\n•\nThe numerical solution advances from a point \u0000 to a \nnew point \u0000 with \u0000\n\n•\nExample\n\n๏\nApply the quadratic Taylor method on [0,1] with h=0.5\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\n(t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n)t\nk+1\n=t\nk\n+h\n!46\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+hx′\u0000\nk\n+\nh\n2\n2\nx′\u0000′\u0000\nk\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nQuadratic Taylor Method (cont’d)\n!47\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+hx′\u0000\nk\n+\nh\n2\n2\nx′\u0000′\u0000\nk\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nQuadratic Taylor Method (Summary)\n•\nThe quadratic Taylor method advances the solution from a point \n(t\nk\n ,x\nk\n ) to a point (t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n) by evaluating the approximate \nTaylor polynomial at x= t\nk+1\n \n•\nThe new value x\nk+1\n is given by\n!48\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nTaylor method of higher degree\n•\nTaylor method of degree p\n!49\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercises\n•\nCompute  numerical  solutions  to  x(1)  for  the  equations  below \nusing two  steps  with  Euler’s  method  and  the  quartic  Taylor \nmethod. For comparison the correct solution to 14 decimal digits \nis given in each case.\n!50\n\nMidpoint Euler and other \nRunge-Kutta methods\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nIntroduction\n•\nTaylor methods\n\n๏\nAdvantage: can attain any approximation order. \n\n๏\nDisadvantage: require symbolic differentiation of the \ndifferential equation (except for Euler’s method).  \n\n•\nNeed to develop some methods of higher order than \nEuler’s method that do not require differentiation of the \ndifferential equation.  \n\n๏\nThey advance from ! to ! by evaluating  ! \nat intermediate  points in the interval !.\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\n(t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n)f(t,x)\n[t\nk\n,t\nk+1\n]\n!52\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint\n•\nSimple improvement of Euler’s method. \n\n•\nThe tangent is a good approximation to a solution curve \nat the initial condition, But \n\n•\nThe quality of the approximation deteriorates as we move \nto the right.\n!53\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint\n•\nSimple improvement of Euler’s method. \n\n•\nThe tangent is a good approximation to a solution curve \nat the initial condition, But \n\n•\nThe quality of the approximation deteriorates as we move \nto the right.\n!53\nImprovement: estimate the slope of each line segment better \n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint (cont’d)\n•\nEuler’s midpoint: two-step procedure which aims to \nestimate the slope at the midpoint between the two \nsolution points. \n\n•\nIn proceeding from \u0000 to \u0000, use the tangent \nto the solution curve at the midpoint \u0000\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\n(t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n)\nt\nk\n+\nh\n2\n!54\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint (cont’d)\n•\nEuler’s midpoint: two-step procedure which aims to \nestimate the slope at the midpoint between the two \nsolution points. \n\n•\nIn proceeding from \u0000 to \u0000, use the tangent to \nthe solution curve at the midpoint \u0000\n\n๏\nCompute an approximation \u0000 to the solution at \u0000 \nusing the traditional Euler’s method. \n\n๏\nDetermine the slope of the solution curve that passes \nthrough the point \u0000 and use this as the slope for a \nstraight line to be followed from \u0000 to \u0000  to determine the \nnew approximation \u0000.\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\n(t\nk+1\n,x\nk+1\n)\nt\nk\n+\nh\n2\nx\nk+\n1\n2\nt\nk\n+\nh\n2\nx\nk+\n1\n2\nt\nk\nt\nk+1\nx\nk+1\n!55\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint (cont’d)\nThe solution is advanced from (t\nk\n, x\nk\n ) to (t\nk \n \n+ h\n, x\nk +1\n) in two steps: \n \nFirst an approximation to the solution is computed at the midpoint \n! by using Euler’s method with step length h/2, \n! \nThen the solution \n \nis advanced \n \nto ! by following the straight line \nfrom ! with slope given by ! \n!       \nt\nk\n+\nh\n2\nx\nk+\n1\n2\n=x\nk\n+\nh\n2\nf(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nt\nk+1\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nf(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk+\n1\n2\n)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+hf(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk+\n1\n2\n)\n!56\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nEuler’s midpoint (cont’d)\n!57\nEuler’s method (h=0.1)\nEuler’s method (h=0.05)\nEuler’s midpoint (h=0.05) \n(dashed)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercise\nConsider the first order differential equation \n! \na)Estimate x(1) by using one step with Euler’s method. \nb)Estimate  x(1)  by  using one  step  with  the  quadratic  Taylor \nmethod. \nc)Estimate x(1) by using two steps with Euler’s midpoint method \n=> h = (b -a)/n where  [a, b] is the interval; a: start point, b: end \npoint \nx′\u0000=x,x(0)=1\n!58\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+hf(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk+\n1\n2\n)\nx\nk+\n1\n2\n=x\nk\n+\nh\n2\nf(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nx1 = x0 + h*x’(0.5, 1.5) = 1 + (1 * 1.5) = 2.5\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n! \nEstimate x(1) by using two steps with Euler’s midpoint method  \n=> h = (b -a)/n where  [a, b] is the interval; a: start point, b: end point \nx\n0\n = 1, h = 0.5 \nx\n1\n=? (approximation of x(0.5)) \nx\n2\n=? (approx. of x(1)) \nx\n0.5\n= x\n0\n + 0.25 * x\n0\n = 1.25 \nx\n1\n = x\n0\n + 0.5*x\n0.5\n = 1 +  0.5 * 1.25 = 1.625 \nx\n1.5\n= x\n1\n + 0.25 * x\n1\n = 1.625 + 0.25*1.625 = 2.03125 \nx\n2\n = x\n1\n + 0.5*x\n1.5\n = 1.625 +  0.5 * 2.03125 = 2.640625 \nx(t) = e\nt  \nx(1) = e\n1\n = 2.71 \n  \n  \nx′\u0000=x,x(0)=1\n!59\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+hf(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk+\n1\n2\n)\nx\nk+\n1\n2\n=x\nk\n+\nh\n2\nf(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nt\n0\n = 0 => x\n0 \napprox x(t\n0\n) = x(0) \nt\n1\n = 0.5 => x\n1 \napprox x(t\n1\n) = x(0.5) \nt\n2\n = 1=> x\n2 \napprox x(t\n2\n) = x(1) \nt\n3 \n=1.5 => x\n3 \napprox x(t\n3\n) = x(1.5)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Runge-Kutta methods\n•\nRunge-Kutta methods are generalizations of the Euler \nmidpoint method. \n\n•\nThe methods use several evaluations of \u0000 between each \nstep which leads to higher accuracy.\n\n•\nGeneralized form\nf\n!60\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤\u0000(t\nk\n,x\nk\n,h)\n\u0000=a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n+...+a\nn\n⇤k\nn\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\nk\n3\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n2\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n21\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h+q\n22\n⇤k\n2\n⇤h)\n.\n.\nk\nn+1\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\nn\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\nn1\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h+q\nn2\n⇤k\n2\n⇤h+...+q\nnn\n⇤k\nn\n⇤h)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\n Runge-Kutta methods\n•\nRunge-Kutta methods are generalizations of the Euler \nmidpoint method. \n\n•\nThe methods use several evaluations of \u0000 between each \nstep which leads to higher accuracy.\n\n•\nGeneralized form\nf\n!60\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤\u0000(t\nk\n,x\nk\n,h)\n\u0000=a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n+...+a\nn\n⇤k\nn\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\nk\n3\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n2\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n21\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h+q\n22\n⇤k\n2\n⇤h)\n.\n.\nk\nn+1\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\nn\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\nn1\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h+q\nn2\n⇤k\n2\n⇤h+...+q\nnn\n⇤k\nn\n⇤h)\nRK-1 ⟺ Euler\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method\n•\nSecond order Runge-Kutta\n\n•\nTaylor method (second order)\n!61\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤f\n0\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nf\n0\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)=f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤x\n0\nt\nf\n0\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)=f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+f\n0\nx\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n))\n1\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n•\nDevelopment using Taylor series\n\n•\nApplying on k\n2\n!62\nf(t+r,x+s)=f(t,x)+r⇤f\n0\nt\n(t,x)+s⇤f\n0\nx\n(t,x)+...\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\u0000\n1\n⇤h⇤f\n0\nt\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+h⇤q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤f\n0\nx\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+O(h\n2\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\u0000\n1\n⇤h⇤f\n0\nt\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+h⇤q\n11\n⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)⇤f\n0\nx\n(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+O(h\n2\n)\nNote:  remove (t\nx\n,x\nk\n) in the rest for abbreviation\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤f+a\n2\n⇤(f+\u0000\n1\n⇤h⇤f\n0\nt\n+h⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n+O(h\n2\n)))\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n3\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\na\n1\n+a\n2\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n=1\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\na\n1\n+a\n2\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n=1\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\na\n1\n+a\n2\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n=1\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\na\n1\n+a\n2\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n=1\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!63\n3\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤f+\nh\n2\n2!\n⇤(f\n0\nt\n+f\n0\nx\n⇤f)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+(a\n1\n+a\n2\n)⇤h⇤f+h\n2\n⇤(a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n⇤f\n0\nt\n+a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n⇤f⇤f\n0\nx\n)+O(h\n3\n)\n2\na\n1\n+a\n2\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤\u0000\n1\n=1\n2⇤a\n2\n⇤q\n11\n=1\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\nHeun\n0\ns method:a\n2\n=\n1\n2\n)a\n1\n=\n1\n2\n,\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=1\nEuler M idpoint method:a\n2\n=1)a\n1\n=0,\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2\nRalston\n0\ns method:a\n2\n=\n2\n3\n)a\n1\n=\n1\n3\n,\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n3\n4\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!64\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!64\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!64\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!64\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\nConsider a\n2\n=\u0000\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤((1\u0000\u0000)⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\u0000⇤f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2⇤\u0000\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2⇤\u0000\n⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n))\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nSecond Order Runge-Kutta Method (cont’d)\n!64\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤(a\n1\n⇤k\n1\n+a\n2\n⇤k\n2\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\u0000\n1\n⇤h,x\nk\n+q\n11\n⇤k\n1\n⇤h)\na\n1\n=1\u0000a\n2\n\u0000\n1\n=q\n11\n=\n1\n2⇤a\n2\nConsider a\n2\n=\u0000\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+h⇤((1\u0000\u0000)⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)+\u0000⇤f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2⇤\u0000\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2⇤\u0000\n⇤f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n))\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nFourth Order Runge-Kutta Method\n•\nGiven the differential equation \u0000  with initial \ncondition \u0000  \n\n•\nThe numerical method given by the formulas is 4\nth\n order \naccurate provided the derivatives of f  up to order four are \ncontinuous and bounded for \u0000  and \u0000 .\nx′\u0000=f(t,x)\nx(a)=x\n0\nt∈[a,b]x∈R\n!65\nk\n0\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2\n⇤k\n0\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2\n⇤k\n1\n)\nk\n3\n=f(t\nk\n+h,x\nk\n+h⇤k\n2\n)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+\nh\n6\n(k\n0\n+2k\n1\n+2k\n2\n+k\n3\n)\n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nExercise\n•\nConsider the first order differential equation: x’= x, x(0)= 1.\n\n•\nEstimate x(1) by using one step with RK 4\nth\n order method.\n\n•\nEstimate x(1) by using two steps with the RK 4\nth\n order method\n!66\nk\n0\n=f(t\nk\n,x\nk\n)\nk\n1\n=f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2\n⇤k\n0\n)\nk\n2\n=f(t\nk\n+\nh\n2\n,x\nk\n+\nh\n2\n⇤k\n1\n)\nk\n3\n=f(t\nk\n+h,x\nk\n+h⇤k\n2\n)\nx\nk+1\n=x\nk\n+\nh\n6\n(k\n0\n+2k\n1\n+2k\n2\n+k\n3\n)\nt\n0 \n= 0 , x\n0\n = 1, x\n1\n=? \nk\n0\n = 1 \nk\n1\n = f(0.5, 1+0.5* 1) = 1.5 \nk\n2\n = f(0.5, 1 + 0.5*1.5)=1.75 \nk\n3\n = f(1, 1 + 1 * 1.75) = 2.75 \nx\n1\n= x\n0\n + (1/6)*(1 + 2*1.5 + 2 *1.75 + 2.75) =  \n=> x\n1\n = 1 + (10.25/6) =2.70833  \n\nNumerical Simulation & ModelingDifferentiation/Derivation\nLab Exercise\n•\nConsider the first order differential equation\n\nx’= f (t ,x) = t\n2\n  + x\n3\n - x, x (0) = 1.\n\n•\nWrite a computer program that implements one of the \nfollowing methods and use it to estimate the value of x(1) \nwith 10, 100, 1000 and 10000 steps?\n\n๏\nEuler’s method.\n\n๏\nQuadratic Taylor method.\n\n๏\nEuler’s midpoint method.\n\n๏\nRunge Kutta fourth order method.\n!67","storage_path":"uploads\\1774207344544_I3344_2021_2022_Part_3.pdf","summary":"Here's a detailed summary of the provided text, organized into bullet points:\n\n## I3344 Numerical Simulation & Modeling\n\n**Instructor:** Ahmad Fadlallah\n\n### Tentative Syllabus Overview\n\n*   **Interpolation and Extrapolation:**\n    *   Linear: Linear regression (`y = ax + b`), correlation, standard deviation.\n    *   Non-linear: k-nearest neighbors (KNN).\n    *   Validation: K-fold cross-validation method for two models.\n*   **Solving Linear Equations:**\n    *   Direct methods: Gauss and LU decomposition.\n    *   Iterative methods: Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel.\n*   **Derivation:**\n    *   Finite Difference Method (FDM): Euler and Runge-Kutta.\n*   **Integration (Surface Estimation):**\n    *   Monte Carlo method.\n    *   Finite Element Method (FEM).\n    *   Comparison of two methods.\n*   **Non-linear Problems:**\n    *   Bisection method.\n*   **Parallel Computing:**\n    *   Introduction to the notion and underlying algorithms.\n\n### Differentiation/Derivation Module Outline\n\n*   What differential equations are.\n*   First order differential equations.\n*   Solving first order equations using Euler’s method.\n*   Analysis of Euler’s method.\n*   Taylor methods.\n*   Euler’s midpoint and Runge-Kutta Methods.\n\n### What are Differential Equations?\n\n*   **Introduction:**\n    *   Essential tool in a wide range of applications.\n    *   Many phenomena can be modeled by a relationship between a function and its derivatives.\n*   **Examples from Physics:**\n    *   **Object in Space:**\n        *   `f(t)`: distance to point P at time `t`.\n        *   Average speed: `[f(t+Δt) - f(t)] / Δt`\n        *   Speed at time `t`: `f'(t)` (derivative of distance).\n        *   Average acceleration: `[v(t+Δt) - v(t)] / Δt`\n        *   Acceleration at time `t`: `v'(t) = f''(t)` (derivative of speed).\n    *   **Newton's Second Law:** `F = ma` (Force equals mass times acceleration).\n        *   Acceleration is proportional to force.\n        *   **Falling Object Example:** Mass `m` falling towards Earth.\n            *   Gravitational force: `F_g = mg`.\n            *   Friction: `F_f = cv^2` (proportional to `v^2`).\n            *   Differential Equation: `F = F_g - F_f = ma => mg - cv^2(t) = mv'(t) => mv' = mg - cv^2`.\n*   **General Use of Differential Equations (4 Steps):**\n    1.  Quantity of interest is modeled by a function `x`.\n    2.  Relation between `x` and its derivatives is derived from known principles (obtaining the differential equation).\n    3.  The differential equation is solved by a mathematical or numerical method.\n    4.  The solution is interpreted in the context of the original problem.\n*   **Different Types of Differential Equations:**\n    *   **First Order:** Involves the unknown function `x` and its first derivative `x'`.\n    *   **P-th Order:** Involves higher derivatives up to order `p`.\n        *   General Linear P-th Order Form: `x^(p)(t) = f(t) + g_0(t)x(t) + g_1(t)x'(t) + ... + g_p(t)x^(p-1)(t)`.\n    *   **Linear:** Equation where the unknown function and its derivatives appear linearly.\n    *   **Ordinary Differential Equations (ODEs):** Unknown function depends on only one variable (e.g., `t`).\n    *   **Partial Differential Equations (PDEs):** Unknown function depends on two or more variables (e.g., coordinates in space).\n\n### First Order Differential Equations\n\n*   **Introduction:**\n    *   Form: `x' = f(t, x)`.\n    *   `x = x(t)` is the unknown function.\n    *   `t` is the free variable.\n    *   Examples: `x'=3`, `x'=2t`, `x'=x`, `x'=t^3+x`, `x'=sin(tx)`.\n*   **Scope:** Derive numerical methods for solving differential equations in the form `x' = f(t, x)`.\n*   **Initial Conditions:**\n    *   Analytical solutions involve a general constant `C`.\n    *   An *initial condition* (`x(a) = x_0`) is needed to specify `C` and obtain a unique solution.\n    *   Example: `x'=2x => x = Ce^(2t)`. If `x(0)=1`, then `C=1 => x(t) = e^(2t)`. If `x(a)=x_0`, then `x(t) = x_0 * e^(2(t-a))`.\n    *   Often has a concrete physical interpretation (e.g., initial speed).\n*   **Definition Revisited:**\n    *   A first order differential equation is `x' = f(t, x)`.\n    *   A specific solution requires an *initial condition* `x(a) = x_0`.\n    *   Complete formulation: `x' = f(t, x), x(a) = x_0`.\n*   **Geometric Interpretation:**\n    *   At any point `(t, x)`, the equation `x' = f(t, x)` prescribes the *slope* of the solution curve passing through that point.\n    *   This leads to the concept of a \"slope field\" or \"direction field.\"\n*   **Conditions of Existence of One Solution:**\n    *   `f(t,x)` should not have problematic behavior (e.g., `x' = sqrt(1-x^2)` requires `-1 <= x <= +1`).\n    *   Ensures existence and uniqueness of solutions.\n*   **General Strategy for Numerical Solution of Differential Equations:**\n    *   **Given:** `x' = f(t, x)`, `x(a) = x_0`, and a solution interval `[a, b]`.\n    *   **Objective:**\n        *   Analytical Solutions: Find the exact function `x(t)`.\n        *   Numerical Solutions: Approximate `x(t)`.\n    *   **Process:**\n        *   Define an increasing sequence of `t`-values: `t_k` for `k=0...n`, with `a = t_0` and `b = t_n`.\n        *   Often, `t_k` values are equally spaced with step length `h`, so `t_k = a + k*h`.\n        *   A numerical method computes a sequence `x_0, x_1, ..., x_n` where `x_k` approximates the true solution `x(t_k)`.\n        *   `x_k` for `k > 0` is computed from previous approximations (`x_k-1`, etc.).\n        *   Continuous approximation can be obtained by connecting neighboring points with straight lines.\n\n### Euler's Method\n\n*   **Introduction:**\n    *   Simplest numerical method.\n    *   Based directly on the geometric interpretation of a first order differential equation.\n*   **Basic Idea and Algorithm:**\n    *   **Aim:** Compute approximations `(t_k, x_k)` to the solution of `x' = f(t, x), x(a) = x_0`.\n    *   **Starting Point:** `(t_0, x_0)` from the initial condition.\n    *   **Step 1 (t_0 to t_1):**\n        *   Compute the slope at `(t_0, x_0)`: `x'_0 = f(t_0, x_0)`.\n        *   The tangent line is `T_0(t) = x_0 + (t - t_0)x'_0`.\n        *   Approximate `x_1` at `t_1 = t_0 + h` using the tangent: `x_1 = T_0(t_1) = x_0 + hx'_0 = x_0 + hf(t_0, x_0)`.\n    *   **General Step (t_k to t_k+1):**\n        *   From `(t_k, x_k)`, compute the derivative `x'_k = f(t_k, x_k)`.\n        *   The tangent at `t_k` is `T_k(t) = x_k + (t - t_k)x'_k = x_k + (t - t_k)f(t_k, x_k)`.\n        *   The approximation at `t_k+1` is: `x_k+1 = x_k + hf(t_k, x_k)`.\n*   **Algorithm (Pseudocode):**\n    *   `h = (b - a) / n`\n    *   `t_0 = a`\n    *   `x_0` is given (initial condition)\n    *   `for k = 0 to n-1:`\n        *   `x_k+1 = x_k + h * f(t_k, x_k)`\n        *   `t_k+1 = a + (k + 1) * h`\n    *   `x_k` will be an approximation to `x(t_k)` for `k=0, ..., n`.\n*   **Geometric Interpretation:**\n    *   Euler's method \"steps between different solution curves\" of `x' = f(t,x)`.\n    *   The \"initial condition\" for the *next* step is effectively updated at each point `(t_k, x_k)`.\n    *   At `(t_k, x_k)`, the tangent to the solution curve *passing through that point* is followed to `(t_k+1, x_k+1)`.\n\n### Error Analysis for Euler's Method\n\n*   **Background:**\n    *   **Partial Derivatives:** `g'_t(t, x)` (derivative w.r.t. `t` holding `x` constant), `g'_x(t, x)` (derivative w.r.t. `x` holding `t` constant).\n    *   **Taylor Polynomial Approximation:** A function `f` with continuous derivatives can be expanded around a point `a`.\n    *   **Mean Value Theorem:** For `g(t, x_2) - g(t, x_1) = g'_x(t, ξ)(x_2 - x_1)` where `ξ` is between `x_1` and `x_2`.\n*   **Local and Global Error:**\n    *   **Basic Idea:** Euler's method approximates `x(t_k+1)` with `x_k+1 = x_k + hf(t_k, x_k)`.\n    *   **Local Truncation Error:** The error introduced in a single step due to approximating a curve with a straight line. This is `O(h^2)` (from Taylor expansion `x(t+h) = x(t) + hx'(t) + (h^2/2)x''(ξ)`).\n    *   **Global Error:** The accumulated error over many steps.\n    *   **Error Relation:** If `f` has continuous first derivatives, the error in Euler's method for `x'=f(t,x)` develops according to a relation that combines:\n        *   The propagation of the global error from the previous step (`k` to `k+1`).\n        *   The local truncation error committed in the current step.\n    *   **Sources of Global Error:**\n        *   Advancement of global error at step `k` to step `k+1`.\n        *   Local truncation error (only two terms in Taylor polynomial).\n    *   **Stability:** Solutions can be stable or unstable, influencing how errors propagate.\n\n### Taylor Methods\n\n*   **Differentiating the Differential Equations:**\n    *   Higher-order derivatives of a solution `x(t)` at a point `a` can be determined by repeatedly differentiating `x'(t) = f(t, x(t))` and substituting known values.\n    *   Example: `x'(t)=t+x^2(t), x(a)=x_0`.\n        *   `x(a)=x_0`, `x'(a)=a+x_0^2`.\n        *   `x''(t) = 1 + 2x(t)x'(t)`.\n        *   `x''(a) = 1 + 2x_0x'(a) = 1 + 2x_0(a+x_0^2)`.\n    *   In general, `x^(p)(a) = F_p(a, x_0)`, where `F_p` is a function of `f` and its derivatives up to order `p-1`.\n*   **Introduction:**\n    *   **Main Idea:** Approximate the solution `x(t+h)` by a Taylor polynomial of a suitable degree.\n    *   Euler's method is the simplest Taylor method (degree 1): `x(t+h) ≈ x(t) + hx'(t)`.\n*   **Quadratic Taylor Method:**\n    *   Approximation: `x(t+h) ≈ x(t) + hx'(t) + (h^2/2)x''(t)`.\n    *   Numerical Solution Step: `x_k+1 = x_k + hx'_k + (h^2/2)x''_k`.\n    *   Requires computing `x''_k` (which depends on `f_t` and `f_x`).\n*   **Taylor Method of Higher Degree `p`:**\n    *   `x_k+1 = x_k + hx'_k + (h^2/2!)x''_k + ... + (h^p/p!)x^(p)_k`.\n    *   Requires computing derivatives of `f(t,x)` up to order `p-1`.\n\n### Midpoint Euler and Other Runge-Kutta Methods\n\n*   **Introduction:**\n    *   **Taylor Method Disadvantage:** Requires symbolic differentiation of the differential equation (except for Euler's).\n    *   **Runge-Kutta Goal:** Develop higher-order methods that *do not* require differentiation of `f(t,x)`.\n    *   **Mechanism:** Advance from `(t_k, x_k)` to `(t_k+1, x_k+1)` by evaluating `f(t,x)` at *intermediate points* within `[t_k, t_k+1]`.\n*   **Euler's Midpoint Method (also known as Modified Euler or RK2):**\n    *   **Simple Improvement:** Estimates the slope at the midpoint of the interval `[t_k, t_k+1]`.\n    *   **Two-Step Procedure to advance from `(t_k, x_k)` to `(t_k+1, x_k+1)`:**\n        1.  **Estimate `x` at midpoint `t_k + h/2`:** Use standard Euler with step `h/2`.\n            *   `x_k+1/2 = x_k + (h/2) * f(t_k, x_k)`\n        2.  **Use midpoint slope to advance to `t_k+1`:** Determine the slope `f(t_k + h/2, x_k+1/2)` and use it to step from `t_k` to `t_k+1`.\n            *   `x_k+1 = x_k + h * f(t_k + h/2, x_k+1/2)`\n*   **Runge-Kutta Methods (RK methods):**\n    *   Generalizations of Euler's midpoint method.\n    *   Achieve higher accuracy by using multiple evaluations of `f(t,x)` between each step.\n    *   **Generalized Form:**\n        *   `x_k+1 = x_k + h * Φ(t_k, x_k, h)`\n        *   `Φ = a_1*k_1 + a_2*k_2 + ... + a_n*k_n` (weighted sum of slopes)\n        *   `k_1 = f(t_k, x_k)`\n        *   `k_2 = f(t_k + c_1*h, x_k + q_11*k_1*h)`\n        *   `k_3 = f(t_k + c_2*h, x_k + q_21*k_1*h + q_22*k_2*h)`\n        *   ...and so on for `k_n+1`.\n    *   **RK-1 is Euler's method.**\n*   **Second Order Runge-Kutta Method (RK2):**\n    *   Matches coefficients with the second-order Taylor method when derived using Taylor series expansions.\n    *   General form for RK2:\n        *   `x_k+1 = x_k + h * (a_1*k_1 + a_2*k_2)`\n        *   `k_1 = f(t_k, x_k)`\n        *   `k_2 = f(t_k + c_1*h, x_k + q_11*k_1*h)`\n    *   Comparing with Taylor series `x_k+1 = x_k + h*f + (h^2/2)*(f'_t + f'_x*f)` leads to a system of equations for `a_1, a_2, c_1, q_11`:\n        *   `a_1 + a_2 = 1`\n        *   `a_2 * c_1 = 1/2`\n        *   `a_2 * q_11 = 1/2`\n    *   Since there are more unknowns than equations, there are families of RK2 methods:\n        *   **Heun's method:** `a_2 = 1/2 => a_1 = 1/2, c_1 = q_11 = 1`.\n        *   **Euler Midpoint method:** `a_2 = 1 => a_1 = 0, c_1 = q_11 = 1/2`.\n        *   **Ralston's method:** `a_2 = 2/3 => a_1 = 1/3, c_1 = q_11 = 3/4`.\n*   **Fourth Order Runge-Kutta Method (RK4):**\n    *   A widely used and highly accurate method.\n    *   **Formulas:**\n        *   `k_0 = f(t_k, x_k)`\n        *   `k_1 = f(t_k + h/2, x_k + (h/2)*k_0)`\n        *   `k_2 = f(t_k + h/2, x_k + (h/2)*k_1)`\n        *   `k_3 = f(t_k + h, x_k + h*k_2)`\n        *   `x_k+1 = x_k + (h/6) * (k_0 + 2*k_1 + 2*k_2 + k_3)`\n    *   It is 4th order accurate, provided derivatives of `f` up to order four are continuous and bounded.\n\n### Lab Exercise\n\n*   **Objective:** Write a computer program to estimate `x(1)` for `x' = t^2 + x^3 - x, x(0) = 1` using varying numbers of steps (10, 100, 1000, 10000) for one of the following methods:\n    *   Euler’s method.\n    *   Quadratic Taylor method.\n    *   Euler’s midpoint method.\n    *   Runge Kutta fourth order method.","timestamp":"2026-03-22 21:22:49"}
