{"file_size":817703,"filename":"I3344-2021-2022-Part-6 (1).pdf","level":"info","message":"Input object for PDFDocument:","mime_type":"application/pdf","original_text":"\n\nI3344\nNumerical Simulation & \nModeling\nAhmad Fadlallah\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nTentative Syllabus\n✓Interpolation and extrapolation\n✓Linear: linear regression y = ax + b; correlation, standard deviation, etc. \n✓Non-linear: k nearest neighbors (KNN)\n✓Validation of two models: k-fold cross validation method.\n✓Solving a linear equation\n✓Direct methods: Gauss and LU\n✓Iterative methods: Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel\n✓Derivation\n✓Finite difference method (FDM): Euler and Runge-kutta\n✓Integration: surface estimation\n✓Finite Element Method (FEM). \n✓Monte Carlo method. \n✓Comparison of two methods.\n✓Non-linear problems\n✓Bisection method\n•Optimization\n•Introduction to the notion of parallel computing and underlying algorithms\n2\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nOutline\n•Introduction\n•Golden Section Search\n•Newton Method\n3\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction\n•Root locationand optimizationare relatedin the sense \nthat both involve guessingand searchingfor a pointon a \nfunction.\n๏Root location: searching for zeros of a function\n๏Optimization: searching for the minimum or the maximum.\n4\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nMathematical Background\n•An optimization or mathematical programming problem \ngenerally be stated as:\nFind x, which minimizesor maximizesf(x) subject to\n•x is an n-dimensional design vector\n•f(x) is the objective function\n•d\ni\n(x) are inequality constraints\n•e\ni\n(x) are equality constraints\n•a\ni\nand b\ni\nare constants\n5\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n6\nOne-\nDimensional \nUnconstraine\nd \nOptimization\nMultidimensio\nnal \nUnconstraine\nd \nOptimization\nConstrained \nOptimization\nOptimization\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n•Multimodalcases: Both localand globaloptimacan \noccur for a single function \n7\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nIntroduction (cont’d)\n•How to distinguish global optimum from local one?\n๏By graphingto gain insight into the behavior of the \nfunction.\n๏Using randomly generated starting guessesand picking \nthe largest of the optimaas global.\n๏Perturbing the starting point associated to a local \noptima to see if the routine returns a better point or the \nsame local optima.\n8\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search\n•A unimodalfunction has a singlemaximum (resp. \nminimum) in the a given interval. For a unimodal \nfunction:\n๏Pick two pointsthat will bracket your extremum [x\nl\n, x\nu\n].\n๏Pick an additional third pointwithin this intervalto \ndetermine whether a maximum occurred.\n๏Pick a fourth pointto determine whether the maximum \nhas occurred within the first three or last three points\n๏The key is making this approach efficient by choosing \nintermediate points wiselythus minimizing the function \nevaluationsby replacing the old values with new values.\n9\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•The sum of the two sub lengths 푙\n1\nand 푙\n2\nmust equal the original interval length.\n•The ratio of the length must be equal\n10\nGolden Ratio\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden Ratio (History)\n11\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•The method starts with two initial \nguesses, 푥\n푙\nand 푥\n푢\n, that bracket \none local extremum of f(x)\n•two interior points 푥\n1\nand 푥\n2\nare \nchosen according to the golden \nratio\n•The function is evaluated at these \ntwo interior points.\n12\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nGolden-Section Search (cont’d)\n•If f(x\n1\n)>f(x\n2\n) \n=> the domain of x to the left of x\n2\nfrom x\nl\nto x\n2 \ndoes not contain the maximum \n=> can be eliminated => new x\nl\n= x\n2\n•If f(x\n2\n)>f(x\n1\n)\n=> the domain of x to the right of x\n1\nfrom x\nl\nto x\n2\n, \nwould have been eliminated => new x\nu\n= x\n1\n•New x\n1\nis determined as before\n13\n•Stop condition: |x\nu\n-x\nl\n| < Ɛ, max = (x\nl\n+ x\nu\n)/2\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use the golden-section search to find the maximum of the \nfollowing function  within the interval x\nl\n=0 and x\nu\n=4.\n•Note\n14\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n15\n•Because f(x\n2\n) > f(x\n1\n), the maximum is in the interval defined by x\nl\n, x\n2\n, and x\n1\n. \n•For the new interval\n•LB remains x\nl\n= 0, x\n1\nbecomes the UB => x\nu\n= 2.472. \n•Former x\n2\nvalue becomes the new x\n1\n, that is, x\n1\n= 1.528.\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution (cont’d)\n16\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nQuadratic Interpolation\n•Parabolic interpolation takes advantage of the fact that a \nsecond-order polynomialoften provides a good \napproximationto the shape of f(x) near an optimum\n17\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nQuadratic Interpolation (cont’d)\n•There is only onequadratic polynomial or parabola\nconnecting three points. \n•if we have three points that jointly bracket an optimum, we \ncan fit a parabola to the points. \n•Then we can differentiate it, set the result equal to zero, and \nsolve for an estimate of the optimal x. \n•It can be shown through some algebraic manipulations that the \nresult is\n•x\n0\n, x\n1\n, and x\n2\nare the initial guesses, and x\n3\nis the value of xthat \ncorresponds to the maximum value of the parabolic fitto the \nguesses\n•and so on (same as golden section search)\n18\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use parabolic interpolation to approximate the maximum \nof the following function  with initial guesses of x\n0\n=0, x\n1=\n1, \nand x\n2 = \n4.\n•Note:\n19\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n20\nBecause the function value for the new point is higher than for the intermediate \npoint (x\n1\n) and the new x value is to the right of the intermediate point, the lower \nguess (x\n0\n) is discarded. Therefore, for the next iteration\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n21\nGolden Section Search\nQuadratic\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nNewton’s Method\n•Based on the Newton-Raphson \nmethod to find the root of an \nequation\n•The Newton-Raphson method is \nbased on the principle that if the \ninitial guess of the root of f(x) = \n0 is at x\ni\n, then if one draws the \ntangent to the curve at f(x\ni\n),the \npoint x\ni+1\nwhere the tangent \ncrosses the x-axis is an \nimproved estimate of the root \n22\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nNewton’s Method (cont’d)\n•Consider a new function g(x)=f'(x). \n•The optimal value x* satisfies: f'(x*)=g(x*)=0\n•We can use the following as a technique to the extremum \nof f(x).\n•Start with an initial guess x\n0\nand repeat the iterations until \nfinding f’(x\nk\n) = 0 (or less than some value)\n23\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\n•Use Newton’s method to approximate the maximum of the \nfollowing function  with initial guesses of x\n0\n=2.5\n•Note:\n24\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise -Solution\n25\n\nDigital Simulation & ModelingInterpolation & Extrapolation\nExercise\nEmploy the following methods to find the maximum of\nf (x) = 4x-1.8x\n2\n+ 1.2x\n3\n-0.3x\n4\n(a) Golden-section search (x\nl\n= -2, x\nu\n= 4, Ɛ\ns\n= 1%).\n(b) Parabolic interpolation (x\n0\n=1.75, x\n1\n=2, x\n2\n=2.5, iter. = 4).\n(c)  Newton’s method (x\n0\n= 3, Ɛ\ns\n=1%).\n26","storage_path":"uploads\\1774214552703_I3344_2021_2022_Part_6__1_.pdf","summary":"Here's a detailed summary of the provided text, covering main ideas, key concepts, important details, definitions, and formulas, organized logically:\n\n**I3344 Numerical Simulation & Modeling**\n*   **Instructor:** Ahmad Fadlallah\n\n**Tentative Course Syllabus Overview**\n*   **Interpolation & Extrapolation:**\n    *   Linear: Linear regression (y = ax + b), correlation, standard deviation.\n    *   Non-linear: k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN).\n*   **Model Validation:** k-fold cross-validation method.\n*   **Solving Linear Equations:**\n    *   Direct methods: Gauss, LU decomposition.\n    *   Iterative methods: Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel.\n*   **Derivation:** Finite Difference Method (FDM) (Euler, Runge-Kutta).\n*   **Integration:** Surface estimation.\n*   **Finite Element Method (FEM).**\n*   **Monte Carlo method.**\n*   **Comparison of methods.**\n*   **Non-linear problems:** Bisection method.\n*   **Optimization.**\n*   **Parallel Computing:** Introduction to notions and algorithms.\n\n---\n\n**Presentation Outline (Focus of Current Material)**\n*   Introduction to Optimization\n*   Golden Section Search\n*   Newton Method (and Quadratic Interpolation implicitly covered)\n\n---\n\n**1. Introduction to Optimization**\n\n*   **Relationship between Root Location and Optimization:**\n    *   Both involve guessing and searching for a point on a function.\n    *   **Root location:** Searching for zeros of a function (f(x) = 0).\n    *   **Optimization:** Searching for the minimum or maximum of a function.\n\n*   **Mathematical Background - Optimization Problem Definition:**\n    *   **Goal:** Find **x** which minimizes or maximizes **f(x)** subject to constraints.\n    *   **x:** An n-dimensional design vector.\n    *   **f(x):** The objective function (the function to be optimized).\n    *   **Constraints:**\n        *   **d_i(x):** Inequality constraints.\n        *   **e_i(x):** Equality constraints.\n        *   **a_i and b_i:** Constants within constraints.\n\n*   **Types of Optimization:**\n    *   One-Dimensional Unconstrained Optimization.\n    *   Multidimensional Unconstrained Optimization.\n    *   Constrained Optimization.\n\n*   **Multimodal Cases:**\n    *   A single function can exhibit both local and global optima (multiple peaks/valleys).\n\n*   **Distinguishing Global Optimum from Local Optima:**\n    *   **Graphing:** Visually inspect the function's behavior.\n    *   **Multiple Starting Guesses:** Use randomly generated starting points and select the best result.\n    *   **Perturbation:** Disturb a local optimum's starting point to check if a better point or the same local optimum is returned.\n\n---\n\n**2. Golden-Section Search**\n\n*   **Applicability:** Designed for **unimodal functions**, which have a single maximum or minimum within a given interval.\n\n*   **Core Idea:** An efficient bracketing method that minimizes function evaluations by strategically choosing intermediate points and replacing old values with new ones.\n\n*   **Process Steps:**\n    1.  **Bracket Extremum:** Start with an interval [x_l, x_u] that is known to contain the extremum.\n    2.  **Intermediate Points:** Pick two interior points (x_1 and x_2) within the interval, determined by the **Golden Ratio**.\n        *   The sum of the two sub-lengths (l1 and l2) must equal the original interval length.\n        *   The ratio of the lengths must be equal, based on the golden ratio (approximately 1.618).\n    3.  **Function Evaluation:** Evaluate f(x_1) and f(x_2).\n    4.  **Interval Reduction (for Maximization):**\n        *   If f(x_1) > f(x_2): The maximum is not to the left of x_2. The new lower bound (x_l) becomes x_2.\n        *   If f(x_2) > f(x_1): The maximum is not to the right of x_1. The new upper bound (x_u) becomes x_1.\n    5.  **Iteration:** A new x_1 (or x_2) is determined for the reduced interval, and the process repeats.\n\n*   **Stop Condition:** The iteration stops when the interval width |x_u - x_l| is less than a predefined tolerance (ε).\n\n*   **Extremum Estimate:** The maximum (or minimum) is estimated as the midpoint of the final interval: (x_l + x_u) / 2.\n\n---\n\n**3. Quadratic Interpolation (Parabolic Interpolation)**\n\n*   **Core Idea:** Approximates the objective function f(x) with a second-order polynomial (parabola) near an optimum, as parabolas often provide a good fit.\n\n*   **Process Steps:**\n    1.  **Initial Points:** Requires three distinct points (x_0, x_1, x_2) that jointly bracket an optimum.\n    2.  **Parabola Fitting:** A unique quadratic polynomial can be fitted through these three points.\n    3.  **Estimate Optimum:** Differentiate the fitted parabola, set the result to zero, and solve for x to estimate the optimal point (x_3).\n    4.  **Formula for x_3 (Estimated Optimum):**\n        -   x_3 = [f(x_0)(x_1^2 - x_2^2) + f(x_1)(x_2^2 - x_0^2) + f(x_2)(x_0^2 - x_1^2)] / [2 * (f(x_0)(x_1 - x_2) + f(x_1)(x_2 - x_0) + f(x_2)(x_0 - x_1))]\n    5.  **Iteration:** Similar to Golden Section Search, an old point is discarded and replaced by the new x_3 to continue the search with a refined triplet of points.\n\n---\n\n**4. Newton's Method**\n\n*   **Basis:** Adapted from the Newton-Raphson method, which is used to find roots of an equation f(x) = 0.\n\n*   **Newton-Raphson for Root Finding:**\n    *   If an initial guess x_i for a root of f(x) = 0 is made, drawing a tangent to f(x) at x_i, the point where the tangent crosses the x-axis (x_{i+1}) is an improved estimate of the root.\n    *   Formula: x_{i+1} = x_i - f(x_i) / f'(x_i)\n\n*   **Application to Optimization:**\n    *   The optimal value x* of f(x) occurs where the first derivative f'(x*) = 0.\n    *   Therefore, Newton's method can be applied to find the root of the function g(x) = f'(x).\n    *   This means replacing f(x) with f'(x) and f'(x) with f''(x) in the original Newton-Raphson formula.\n\n*   **Iterative Formula for Optimization:**\n    *   x_{k+1} = x_k - f'(x_k) / f''(x_k)\n        *   x_k: Current guess for the optimum.\n        *   f'(x_k): First derivative of the objective function at x_k.\n        *   f''(x_k): Second derivative of the objective function at x_k.\n\n*   **Stop Condition:** The iteration continues until f'(x_k) is approximately zero (or less than a specified tolerance).\n\n---\n\n**Exercise Summary**\n\n*   The presentation concludes with an exercise to apply all three methods (Golden-section search, Parabolic interpolation, Newton's method) to find the maximum of the function `f(x) = 4x - 1.8x^2 + 1.2x^3 - 0.3x^4` with specific initial conditions and stopping criteria.","timestamp":"2026-03-22 23:22:51"}
