{"file_size":1736788,"filename":"I3304-2023-2024-Part-7 (1).pdf","level":"info","message":"Input object for PDFDocument:","mime_type":"application/pdf","original_text":"\n\nI3304\nNetwork administration and \nsecurity\nAhmad Fadlallah\n1\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nReferenceTextbooks\n2\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nOutline\n•Introduction \n๏Introduction to the course\n๏Recall Network Basics (I2208)\n•Network  Layer\n๏Static Routing\n๏Dynamic Routing\n-Dynamic Routing Algorithm\n-Dynamic Routing Protocols\n๏NAT (Network Address Translation)\n๏IPv6\n•Transport Layer\n๏Function of the transport layer\n๏UDP Protocol\n๏TCP Protocol\n-Connection management\n-Flow control\n-Congestion control\n•Application Layer\nHTTP protocol\nFTP protocol\nTelnet\nMail protocols\nDNS\nP2P Applications\n•Introduction to Security\nSecurity services\nCryptography\nDigital Signature\nPrinciple of network security \nprotocols\n3\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nReferences\n•The slides are based on the:\n๏Jim Kurose, Keith Ross Slides for the Computer Networking: A Top-Down \nApproach, 8th edition, Pearson, 2020\n๏Cisco Networking Academy Program, Routing and Switching Essentials v6.0\n4\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nIntroduction to Security: Outline\n•Security services\n•Cryptography\n•Digital Signature\n•Principle of network security protocols\n5\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nDefinitions\n•Computer Security:generic name for the collection of tools designed to \nprotect data and to thwart hackers\n•Network Security :measures to protect data during their transmission\n•Internet Security/ Cybersecurity: measures to protect data during their \ntransmission over a collection of interconnected networks i.e., in the \ncyberspace\n•Information Security: involves the protection of data across all mediums\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nOSI Security Architecture\n•ITU-T X.800 “Security Architecture for OSI”\n•Defines a systematic way of defining and providing security \nrequirements\n•for us it provides a useful, if abstract, overview of concepts we will study\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nAspects of Security\n•3 aspects of information security:\n๏security attacks (and threats)\n-actions that (may) compromise security\n๏security services\n-services counter to attacks\n๏security mechanisms\n-used by services\n-E.g., secrecy is a service, encryption (a.k.a. encipherment) is a mechanism\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSecurity Attack\n•Any action that compromises the security of information owned by an \norganization\n•Information security is about how to prevent attacks, or failing that, to \ndetect attacks on information-based systems\n•Often threat & attack used to mean same thing But, there is a \ndifference...\n•have a wide range of attacks\n•can focus of generic types of attacks\n๏passive\n๏active\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nPassive Attacks\n•interception of the messages \n•What can the attacker do?\n๏read the content\n๏traffic analysis\n-hard to avoid\n•Hard to detect, try to prevent\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nActive attacks\n•Attacker actively manipulates the \ncommunication\n•Masquerade\n๏pretend as someone else\n๏possible to get more privileges \n•Fabrication\n๏create a bogus message\n•Replay\n๏passively capture data and send later\n•Denial-of-service\n๏prevention the normal use of \nservers, end users, or network itself\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nActive attacks\n•deny\n๏repudiate sending/receiving a message later\n•modification\n๏change the content of a message\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSecurity Service\n•Enhance security of data processing systems and information transfers of \nan organization\n•Intended to counter security attacks\n•Using one or more security mechanisms \n•Often replicates functions normally associated with physical documents\n๏which, for example, have signatures, dates; need protection from \ndisclosure, tampering, or destruction; be notarized or witnessed; be \nrecorded or licensed\n•X.800:\n“a service provided by a protocol layer of communicating open systems, \nwhich ensures adequate security of the systems or of data transfers”\n•RFC 2828:\n“a processing or communication service provided by a system to give a \nspecific kind of protection to system resources”\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nBasic Security Services\n•Authentication\n๏assurance that the communicating entity is the one it claims to be\n๏peer entity authentication \n-mutual confidence in the identities of the parties involved in a connection\n๏Data-origin authentication\n-assurance about the source of the received data\n•Access Control\n๏prevention of the unauthorized use of a resource\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nBasic Security Services\n•Data Confidentiality\n๏protection of data from unauthorized disclosure (against eavesdropping)\n๏traffic flow confidentiality is one step ahead\n•Data Integrity\n๏assurance that data received are exactly as sent by an authorized sender\n๏i.e. no modification, insertion, deletion, or replay\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nBasic Security Services\n•Non-Repudiation\n๏protection against denial by one of the parties in a communication\n๏Origin non-repudiation\n-proof that the message was sent by the specified party\n๏Destination non-repudiation\n-proof that the message was received by the specified party\n•Availability\n๏The property of a system or a system resource being accessible and usable \nupon demand by an authorized system entity\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nFriends and enemies: Alice, Bob, Trudy\n•well-known in network security world\n•Bob, Alice want to communicate “securely”\n•Trudy (intruder) may intercept, delete, add messages\nsecure\nsender\nsecure\nreceiver\nchannel\ndata, control \nmessages\ndata\ndata\nAlice\nBob\nTrudy\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nFriends and enemies: Alice, Bob, Trudy\nWho might Bob and Alice be?\n•... well, real-lifeBobs and Alices!\n•Web browser/server for electronic transactions (e.g., on-line \npurchases)\n•on-line banking client/server\n•DNS servers\n•BGP routers exchanging routing table updates\n•other examples?\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nThe language of cryptography\nmplaintext message\nK\nA\n(m) ciphertext, encrypted with key K\nA\nm = K\nB\n(K\nA\n(m))\nplaintext\nplaintext\nciphertext\nK\nA\nencryption\nalgorithm\ndecryption \nalgorithm\nAlice’s \nencryption\nkey\nBob’s \ndecryption\nkey\nK\nB\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSymmetric key cryptography\nsymmetric key crypto: Bob and Alice share same (symmetric) \nkey: 퐾\n푠\n•e.g., key is knowing substitution pattern in mono alphabetic \nsubstitution cipher\nQ:how do Bob and Alice agree on key value?\nplaintext\nciphertext\nK\nS\nencryption\nalgorithm\ndecryption \nalgorithm\nK\nS\nplaintext\nmessage, m\nK    (m)\nS\nm = K\nS\n(K\nS\n(m))\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSimple encryption scheme\nsubstitution cipher: substituting one thing for another\nmonoalphabetic cipher: substitute one letter for another\nplaintext:  abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\nciphertext:  mnbvcxzasdfghjklpoiuytrewq\nPlaintext: bob. i love you. alice\nciphertext: nkn. s gktc wky. mgsbc\ne.g.:\nEncryption key: mapping from set of 26 letters  \nto set of 26 letters\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSymmetric key crypto: DES\nDES: Data Encryption Standard\n•US encryption standard [NIST 1993]\n•56-bit symmetric key, 64-bit plaintext input\n•block cipher with cipher block chaining\n•how secure is DES?\n•making DES more secure:\n๏3DES: encrypt 3 times with 3 different keys\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSymmetric key \ncrypto: DES\ninitial permutation \n16 identical “rounds”of \nfunction application, each \nusing different 48 bits of \nkey\nfinal permutation\nDES operation\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nAES: Advanced Encryption Standard\n•symmetric-key NIST standard, replaced DES (Nov 2001)\n•processes data in 128 bit blocks\n•128, 192, or 256 bit keys\n•brute force decryption (try each key) taking less than 1 sec on DES, \ntakes 149 trillion years for AES\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nPublic Key Cryptography\nsymmetric key crypto\n•requires sender, receiver \nknow shared secret key\n•Q: how to agree on key in \nfirst place (particularly if \nnever “met”)?\npublic key crypto\nradically different \napproach [Diffie-\nHellman76, RSA78]\nsender, receiver do not\nshare secret key\npublicencryption key \nknown toall\nprivatedecryption key \nknown only to receiver\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nPublic Key Cryptography\nm = K  (K  (m))\nB\n+\nB\n-\nplaintext\nencryption\nalgorithm\ndecryption \nalgorithm\nK  (m)\nB\n+\nciphertext\nplaintext\nmessage, m\nK \nB\n+\nBob’s publickey \nBob’s privatekey \nK \nB\n-\nWow-public key cryptography revolutionized 2000-year-old (previously \nonly symmetric key) cryptography!\n•similar ideas emerged at roughly same time, independently in US and UK (classified)\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nPublic key encryption algorithms\nrequirements:\nRSA: Rivest, Shamir, Adelson algorithm\n1\nneed K  ( ) and K  ( ) such that\nB\nB\n.\n.\n+\n-\nK  (K   (m))  =  m \nB\nB\n-\n+\ngiven public key K  , it should be impossible to \ncompute private key K  \nB\nB\n2\n+\n-\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nPublic key cryptography\nplaintext\nmessage, m\nciphertext\nencryption\nalgorithm\ndecryption \nalgorithm\nBob’s public \nkey \nplaintext\nmessage\nK  (m)\nB\n+\nK \nB\n+\nBob’s private\nkey \nK \nB\n-\nm = K  (K  (m))\nB\n+\nB\n-\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nDigital signatures \ncryptographic technique analogous to hand-written signatures:\nsender (Bob) digitally  signs document: he is document owner/creator. \nverifiable, nonforgeable:recipient (Alice) can prove to someone that \nBob, and no one else (including Alice), must have signed document \nsimple digital signature for message m:\n•Bob signs m by encrypting with his private key K\nB\n, creating “signed” message, K\nB\n-\n(m)\nBob’s message, m\nPublic  key\nencryption\nalgorithm\nBob’s private\nkey \nK \nB\n-\nm,K \nB\n-\n(m)\nDear Alice\nOh, how I have missed \nyou. I think of you all the \ntime! ...(blah blah blah)\nBob\nDear Alice\nOh, how I have missed \nyou. I think of you all the \ntime! ...(blah blah blah)\nBob\nK \nB\n-\n(m)\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nDigital signatures \n-\nAlice thus verifies that:\nBob signed m\nno one else signed m\nBob signed m and not m’\nnon-repudiation:\nAlice can take m, and signature K\nB\n(m) to court and prove that Bob \nsigned m\n-\nsuppose Alice receives msg m, with signature: m, K\nB\n(m)\nAlice verifies m signed by Bob by applying Bob’s public key K\nB\nto K\nB\n(m) \nthen checks K\nB\n(K\nB\n(m) ) = m.\nIf K\nB\n(K\nB\n(m) ) = m, whoever signed m must have used Bob’s private key\n-\n-\n-\n+\n+\n+\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nMessage digests\nHash function properties:\nmany-to-1\nproduces fixed-size msg digest (fingerprint)\ngiven message digest x, computationally  infeasible to find msuch that x = \nH(m)\nlarge \nmessage\nm\nH: Hash\nFunction\nH(m)\ncomputationally expensive to public-key-encrypt long messages \ngoal: fixed-length, easy-to-compute digital “fingerprint”\napply hash function H to m, get fixed size message digest, H(m)\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nInternet checksum: poor crypto hash function\nInternet checksum has some properties of hash function:\nproduces fixed length digest (16-bit sum) of message\nis many-to-one\nbut given message with given hash value, it is easy to find another \nmessage with same hash value: \nI O U 1\n0 0 . 9\n9 B O B\n49 4F 55 31\n30 30 2E 39\n39 42 D2 42\nmessage\nASCII format\nB2 C1 D2 AC\nI O U 9\n0 0 . 1\n9 B O B\n49 4F 55 39\n30 30 2E 31\n39 42 D2 42\nmessage\nASCII format\nB2 C1 D2 AC\ndifferent messages\nbut identical checksums!\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nDigital signature = signed message digest\ndigital\nsignature\n(encrypt)\n+\nBob sends digitally signed message:\nlarge \nmessage\nm\nH: Hash\nFunction\nH(m)\nAlice verifies signature, integrity \nof digitally signed message:\nH: Hash\nfunction\nH(m)\nH(m\n)\nlarge \nmessage\nm\nBob’s \nprivate\nkey \nK \nB\n-\nK\nB\n(H(m))\n-\nencrypted \nmessage digest\nK\nB\n(H(m))\n-\nencrypted \nmessage digest\ndigital\nsignature\n(decrypt)\nBob’s \npublic\nkey \nK \nB\n+\n?\nequal\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nHash function algorithms\n•MD5 hash function widely used (RFC 1321) \n๏computes 128-bit message digest in 4-step process. \n๏arbitrary 128-bit string x, appears difficult to construct msg m whose \nMD5 hash is equal to x\n•SHA-1 is also used\n๏US standard [NIST, FIPS PUB 180-1]\n๏160-bit message digest\nSecurity\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSSL: Secure Sockets Layer\n•widely deployed security protocol\n๏supported by almost all browsers, web \nservers\n๏https\n๏billions $/year over SSL\n•mechanisms: [Woo 1994], \nimplementation: Netscape\n•variation -TLS: transport layer \nsecurity, RFC 2246\n•provides\n๏confidentiality\n๏integrity\n๏authentication\n•original goals:\n๏Web e-commerce transactions \n๏encryption (especially credit-\ncard numbers)\n๏Web-server authentication\n๏optional client authentication\n๏minimum hassle in doing \nbusiness with new merchant\n•available to all TCP \napplications\n๏secure socket interface\n8-35\nSecurity\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nSSL and TCP/IP\n•SSL provides application programming interface (API) to applications\n•C and Java SSL libraries/classes readily available\nApplication\nTCP\nIP\nnormal application\nApplication\nSSL\nTCP\nIP\napplication  with SSL\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nToy SSL: a simple secure channel\n•Handshake: Alice and Bob use their certificates, private keys to \nauthenticate each other and exchange shared secret\n•Key derivation: Alice and Bob use shared secret to derive set of keys\n•Data transfer: data to be transferred is broken up into series of records\n•Connection closure: special messages to securely close connection\n\nI3304 -Ne twork Administration and Security2023-2024\nReal SSL\nconnection\nTCP FIN follows\neverything\nhenceforth\nis encrypted","storage_path":"uploads\\1774217444307_I3304_2023_2024_Part_7__1_.pdf","summary":"Here's a detailed summary of the provided text, covering main ideas, key concepts, important details, and definitions:\n\n**I3304: Network Administration and Security (Ahmad Fadlallah)**\n\n*   **Course Overview:**\n    *   This document outlines the content for the I3304 - Network Administration and Security course for 2023-2024.\n\n*   **Reference Textbooks:**\n    *   The slides are based on \"The Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, 8th edition\" by Jim Kurose and Keith Ross.\n    *   Also references the \"Cisco Networking Academy Program, Routing and Switching Essentials v6.0.\"\n\n*   **Course Outline:**\n    *   **Introduction:**\n        *   Introduction to the course.\n        *   Recall Network Basics (I2208).\n    *   **Network Layer:**\n        *   Static Routing.\n        *   Dynamic Routing:\n            *   Dynamic Routing Algorithm.\n            *   Dynamic Routing Protocols.\n        *   NAT (Network Address Translation).\n        *   IPv6.\n    *   **Transport Layer:**\n        *   Function of the transport layer.\n        *   UDP Protocol.\n        *   TCP Protocol:\n            *   Connection management.\n            *   Flow control.\n            *   Congestion control.\n    *   **Application Layer:**\n        *   HTTP protocol.\n        *   FTP protocol.\n        *   Telnet.\n        *   Mail protocols.\n        *   DNS.\n        *   P2P Applications.\n    *   **Introduction to Security:**\n        *   Security services.\n        *   Cryptography.\n        *   Digital Signature.\n        *   Principle of network security protocols.\n\n*   **Introduction to Security: Detailed Outline**\n    *   Security services.\n    *   Cryptography.\n    *   Digital Signature.\n    *   Principle of network security protocols.\n\n*   **Key Definitions (Security):**\n    *   **Computer Security:** Generic term for tools protecting data and thwarting hackers.\n    *   **Network Security:** Measures to protect data during transmission.\n    *   **Internet Security/Cybersecurity:** Measures to protect data during transmission over interconnected networks (cyberspace).\n    *   **Information Security:** Protection of data across all mediums.\n\n*   **OSI Security Architecture (ITU-T X.800):**\n    *   Defines a systematic way to specify and provide security requirements.\n    *   Provides an abstract overview of security concepts.\n\n*   **Aspects of Security:**\n    *   **Security Attacks (and Threats):** Actions that may compromise security.\n    *   **Security Services:** Counter to attacks.\n    *   **Security Mechanisms:** Tools used by services (e.g., encryption for secrecy).\n\n*   **Security Attack:**\n    *   Any action compromising an organization's information security.\n    *   Focuses on preventing or detecting attacks on information-based systems.\n    *   Categorized into passive and active attacks.\n\n*   **Passive Attacks:**\n    *   **Nature:** Interception of messages without altering them.\n    *   **Attacker Actions:** Reading content, traffic analysis.\n    *   **Characteristics:** Hard to detect, focus on prevention.\n\n*   **Active Attacks:**\n    *   **Nature:** Attacker actively manipulates communication.\n    *   **Types:**\n        *   **Masquerade:** Pretending to be someone else, potentially gaining privileges.\n        *   **Fabrication:** Creating bogus messages.\n        *   **Replay:** Capturing data and resending it later.\n        *   **Denial-of-Service (DoS):** Preventing normal use of servers, end-users, or the network.\n        *   **Deny (Repudiation):** A party later denying sending or receiving a message.\n        *   **Modification:** Changing the content of a message.\n\n*   **Security Service:**\n    *   **Purpose:** Enhance the security of data processing systems and information transfers.\n    *   **Method:** Intended to counter security attacks using one or more security mechanisms.\n    *   **Analogy:** Replicates functions of physical documents (signatures, dates, protection from disclosure/tampering).\n    *   **Definitions:**\n        *   X.800: \"a service provided by a protocol layer of communicating open systems, which ensures adequate security of the systems or of data transfers.\"\n        *   RFC 2828: \"a processing or communication service provided by a system to give a specific kind of protection to system resources.\"\n\n*   **Basic Security Services:**\n    *   **Authentication:** Assurance of entity identity.\n        *   **Peer Entity Authentication:** Mutual confidence in identities of communicating parties.\n        *   **Data-Origin Authentication:** Assurance about the source of received data.\n    *   **Access Control:** Prevention of unauthorized use of resources.\n    *   **Data Confidentiality:** Protection of data from unauthorized disclosure (eavesdropping).\n        *   **Traffic Flow Confidentiality:** One step beyond basic data confidentiality.\n    *   **Data Integrity:** Assurance that data received is exactly as sent by an authorized sender (no modification, insertion, deletion, replay).\n    *   **Non-Repudiation:** Protection against denial by a party in communication.\n        *   **Origin Non-Repudiation:** Proof that a message was sent by the specified party.\n        *   **Destination Non-Repudiation:** Proof that a message was received by the specified party.\n    *   **Availability:** A system/resource being accessible and usable on demand by an authorized entity.\n\n*   **Alice, Bob, Trudy Analogy:**\n    *   **Alice & Bob:** Want to communicate securely.\n    *   **Trudy (Intruder):** May intercept, delete, or add messages.\n    *   **Real-world Examples:** Web browser/server transactions, online banking, DNS servers, BGP routers.\n\n*   **The Language of Cryptography:**\n    *   **m:** Plaintext message.\n    *   **K_A(m):** Ciphertext, message 'm' encrypted with key K_A.\n    *   **m = K_B(K_A(m)):** Decryption reverses encryption.\n    *   Involves plaintext, ciphertext, encryption/decryption algorithms, and keys (Alice's encryption key, Bob's decryption key).\n\n*   **Symmetric Key Cryptography:**\n    *   **Concept:** Bob and Alice share the *same* secret key (K_s).\n    *   **Mechanism:** Same key used for both encryption and decryption (m = K_s(K_s(m))).\n    *   **Example:** Monoalphabetic substitution cipher (key is the substitution pattern).\n    *   **Challenge:** How to agree on the shared key value.\n    *   **DES (Data Encryption Standard):**\n        *   US encryption standard (NIST 1993).\n        *   56-bit symmetric key, 64-bit plaintext input.\n        *   Block cipher with cipher block chaining.\n        *   Operation involves an initial permutation, 16 identical \"rounds\" using different 48-bit key segments, and a final permutation.\n        *   **3DES:** Encrypts data three times with three different keys to enhance security.\n    *   **AES (Advanced Encryption Standard):**\n        *   Replaced DES as NIST standard (Nov 2001).\n        *   Processes data in 128-bit blocks.\n        *   Supports 128, 192, or 256-bit keys.\n        *   Significantly more secure than DES against brute-force attacks.\n\n*   **Public Key Cryptography:**\n    *   **Concept:** Radically different approach; sender and receiver *do not* share a secret key.\n    *   **Keys:**\n        *   **Public Encryption Key:** Known to everyone.\n        *   **Private Decryption Key:** Known only to the receiver.\n    *   **Mechanism:** m = K_B^-(K_B^+(m)) (Bob's private key decrypts what his public key encrypted).\n    *   **Significance:** Revolutionized cryptography.\n    *   **RSA (Rivest, Shamir, Adelson) Algorithm:**\n        *   **Requirements:**\n            1.  K_B^-(K_B^+(m)) = m (decryption reverses encryption).\n            2.  Given the public key K_B^+, it should be computationally impossible to compute the private key K_B^-.\n\n*   **Digital Signatures:**\n    *   **Concept:** Cryptographic technique analogous to handwritten signatures.\n    *   **Properties:** Verifiable, non-forgeable (recipient can prove sender's identity and that no one else signed it).\n    *   **Simple Digital Signature for Message 'm':**\n        *   Sender (Bob) signs 'm' by encrypting it with his private key K_B^-, creating K_B^-(m).\n        *   Receiver (Alice) verifies by applying Bob's public key K_B^+ to K_B^-(m).\n        *   If K_B^+(K_B^-(m)) = m, it confirms Bob's private key was used.\n    *   **Non-Repudiation:** Alice can use the message and signature in court to prove Bob signed 'm'.\n\n*   **Message Digests (Hash Functions):**\n    *   **Purpose:** To create a fixed-length, easy-to-compute digital \"fingerprint\" of a large message. Computationally expensive to public-key-encrypt long messages directly.\n    *   **Properties:**\n        *   Many-to-one mapping (different messages can produce the same digest, but collision resistance is key).\n        *   Produces a fixed-size message digest (fingerprint).\n        *   Given a message digest 'x', it is computationally infeasible to find a message 'm' such that x = H(m) (preimage resistance).\n    *   **Internet Checksum (Poor Crypto Hash Function):**\n        *   Produces fixed-length (16-bit) digest and is many-to-one.\n        *   **Weakness:** Easy to find different messages with the same checksum value.\n    *   **Digital Signature using Message Digest:**\n        *   Sender (Bob) applies a hash function H to 'm' to get H(m).\n        *   Bob encrypts H(m) with his private key K_B^- to create the signed message digest: K_B^-(H(m)).\n        *   Alice receives 'm' and K_B^-(H(m)).\n        *   Alice computes H(m) from the received message.\n        *   Alice decrypts the received signature using Bob's public key K_B^+ to get K_B^+(K_B^-(H(m))) = H(m)'.\n        *   Alice compares her computed H(m) with the decrypted H(m)'. If they match, the signature is verified, and integrity is confirmed.\n    *   **Hash Function Algorithms:**\n        *   **MD5 (RFC 1321):** Computes a 128-bit message digest. Difficult to construct a message for a given MD5 hash.\n        *   **SHA-1 (NIST, FIPS PUB 180-1):** US standard, computes a 160-bit message digest.\n\n*   **SSL (Secure Sockets Layer):**\n    *   **Description:** Widely deployed security protocol, supported by most browsers and web servers (e.g., HTTPS).\n    *   **Evolution:** A variation is TLS (Transport Layer Security, RFC 2246).\n    *   **Provides:** Confidentiality, integrity, authentication.\n    *   **Original Goals:** Secure web e-commerce transactions (especially credit card numbers), web-server authentication, optional client authentication, ease of doing business with new merchants.\n    *   **Availability:** Provided to all TCP applications via a secure socket interface (API).\n    *   **Position in Stack:** Sits between the Application and TCP layers (Application -> SSL -> TCP -> IP).\n    *   **\"Toy SSL\" (Simplified Channel Process):**\n        *   **Handshake:** Alice and Bob use certificates and private keys for mutual authentication and to exchange a shared secret.\n        *   **Key Derivation:** Alice and Bob use the shared secret to derive a set of keys.\n        *   **Data Transfer:** Data is broken into records and transferred encrypted.\n        *   **Connection Closure:** Special messages are used to securely close the connection.","timestamp":"2026-03-23 00:11:02"}
