{"file_size":312859,"filename":"PHP_Chapter4_Eng_Cookies.pdf","level":"info","message":"Input object for PDFDocument:","mime_type":"application/pdf","original_text":"\n\nAli JABER\n   ali.jabber@ul.edu.lb\n     Computer Science Department,\n     Faculty of Sciences\nPHP\n Pre HyperText  \n  Processor\n1\n\nChapter4\nCookies\n2\n\nDefinition and Example\n◼What is a Cookie?\nA cookie is often used to identify a user. A cookie is a small file that the server embeds on \nthe user's computer. Each time the same computer requests a page with a browser, it will \nsend the cookie too. With PHP, you can both create and retrieve cookie values.\nA cookie is a small file that is saved on the visitor's computer. This file contains text and is \nused to “remember” information about the visitor. For example, you enter the visitor's \nnickname (Login, Password, etc.) in a cookie, so the next time they come to your site you \ncan read their nickname by looking at what their cookie contains.\n3\n◼Create Cookies With PHP\nA cookie is created with the setcookie() function. \nSyntax:setcookie(name,value,expire,path,domain,secure,httponly);\nOnly thenameparameter is required. All other parameters are optional.\n\nImportant Notes \n◼You must call setcookie() before any <HTML> or <HEAD> \ntag because cookies must pass before other in headers. \n◼The cookie will only be accessible before loading the next \npage, or reloading of the current page: \n❑when the browser calls a page via HTTP, it looks for valid \ncookies for that page and if so, sends them to the HTTP \nrequest header. \n4\n\nDefinition and Example\n<?php\n   $cookie_name =\"user\";\n   $cookie_value= \"Ali Jaber\";\n   setcookie($cookie_name, $cookie_value, time() + (86400*30),\"/\");\n      // 86400 = 60*60*24 = 1 day\n?>\n<html>\n<head><title>session cookie</title></head>\n<body>\n<?php\n   if(!isset($_COOKIE[$cookie_name])) {\n     echo\"Cookie named \". $cookie_name .\" is not set!\";\n    }\n    else{\necho\"Cookie \". $cookie_name .\" is set!<br>\";\necho\"Value is: \".$_COOKIE[$cookie_name];\n   }\n?>\n</body>\n</html>\n5\n\nDefinition and Example\n◼Cookie = small information stored in the browser of the client \nmachine.\n<?php\nif (isset($_COOKIE['counter']))       \nsetcookie(\"counter\", $_COOKIE['counter']+1);\n// be careful the first time counter is not defined!\nelse\nsetcookie(\"counter\",1);\n?>\n<html>\n<head><title>session cookie</title></head>\n<body>\n<p>\nthe page wasexecutedvisited<?phpecho$_COOKIE['counter'];?> times.\n</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n6\n\nExplanations \n◼setcookie defines a cookie that will be sent with the rest of the HTTP \ncommunication headers\n◼The previous script shows the number of times a page has been visited \nsince we started a browser session! \n◼At each execution, the counter is incremented by 1. \n◼If we stop the browser and restart it, a new session restarts. \n◼$ _COOKIE is a superglobal variable (here, an array), which contains \nthe cookies transmitted during the HTTP request. \n◼$ _COOKIE['cookie_name'] returns the value of cookie_name (in the \nexample 'counter'). \n◼Warning! the first time, during the setcookie, $ _COOKIE is not \nassigned the cookie sent to the customer! \n7\n\nsetcookie \nsetcookie (name, value, expiration_date, path, domain, secure, httponly) \n◼\"name\" is the only mandatory argument\n◼\"expiration_date\": expiration date of cookie (in seconds)\n❑a past date allows to delete a cookie\n❑without, the cookie is destroyed when the browser is closed: it is a session cookie. \n◼\"path\" = path on the server where the cookie will be available. \n❑If the value is '/', it will be available on the entire domain “domain” \n❑If the value is '/rep/', it will only be available in the /rep/ directory as well as subdirectories like \n'/rep/rep1/' for example. domain “domain” . \n❑The default value is the current directory where the cookie has been defined. \n◼\"domain\" = domain where the cookie is available \n❑\".u-picardie.fr\" indicates that all pages of the sub-domains of u-picardie.fr can read the cookie \n❑without, it is the current domain \n◼'secure' : a boolean value (0 or 1) indicating whether access to the cookie is protected, \ntherefore only communicated by HTTPS.\n◼\"httponly\" : If set to true, the cookie is only available over the HTTP protocol. Scripting \nlanguages like JavaScript will not be able to access the cookie. \n8\n\nCookie with Expiry Date \n<?php\n$expiry=2;\nif ( isset($_POST['submit']) ) setcookie(\"counter2\",0,time()-3600);  \nelseif ( isset($_COOKIE[‘times']) )\nsetcookie(\"times\",$_COOKIE['times']+1, time()+60*60*24*$expiry);\nelsesetcookie(\"times\",1, time()+60*60*24*$expiry);\n?>\n<html> <head> <title> cookie: expiration </title> </head>\n<body>\n<?php\nif ( isset($_POST['submit']) ) echo\"expiration of cookie !\";  \nelseecho'the  page wasexecutedvisited‘\n.(isset($_COOKIE['times'])?$_COOKIE['times']:0).' fois.<br>'\n.'<formmethod=\"post\" action=\"cookie2.php\">'\n.'<input type=\"submit\" name=\"submit\" '.' value=\"Expiry\"> </form>' ;\n?>\n</body></html>\n9\n\nCookie with Expiry Date\nThe page has been visited0 times.\nexpiry\nThe page has been visited 1 times.\nexpiry\nThe page has been visited 2 times.\nexpiry\nExpiration date of the cookie: \n•time()+60*60*24*$expiry at the current date \nplus 2 days.\n•time()-3600 allows to \"expiry\" the cookie.\n•the cookie is only accessible when loading the \nnext page, a test avoids the error message! \nClose all the browser windows to stop it and restart the browser with cookie2.php: \na new session restarts ....\nWeclick on \nexpiry\nexpiration of cookie!\nThe page has been visited 3 times.\nexpiry\n10\n\nDelete a Cookie\n<?php\n   // set the expiration date to one hour ago\n    setcookie(\"user\",\"\", time() -3600);\n?>\n<html>\n<body>\n<?php\n    echo\"Cookie 'user' is deleted.\";\n?>\n</body>\n</html>\n11\nDelete a cookie: To delete a cookie, use the setcookie() function with an \nexpiration date in the past\n\nModify a Cookie\n12\nTo modify a cookie, simply set (again) the cookie using the setcookie() \nfunction:\n<?php\nif ( isset($_COOKIE [\"User\"])  && ($_COOKIE [\"User\"] == \"Ali\")) \n{\n// change the value of $_COOKIE[\"User\"]\nsetcookie(\"User\", \"Jaber\",time()+3600*30); // validfor one month\n} \n?>\n\nCheck if Cookies are Enabled\n13\nThe following example creates a small script that checks if cookies are \nenabled. First, try creating a test cookie with the setcookie() function, then \ncount the $_COOKIE array variable:\n<?php\n   setcookie(\"test_cookie\",\"test\", time() +3600,'/');\n?>\n<html>\n<body>\n<?php\n     if(count($_COOKIE) >0) {\n        echo\"Cookies are enabled.\";\n     }else{\n        echo\"Cookies are disabled.\";\n        }\n?>\n</body>\n</html>\n\nCookie: Example\n14\nSo what does the code above do? \n1.The first parameter sets the cookie name as \"w3resource\"\n2.The second parameter sets the value as\"w3resource tutorials\" \n3.The third parameter indicates that the cookie will expire after 3600 seconds\n4.The fourth parameter sets the path to the server /home /your_name where \nyour_name can be a username\n5.The fifth parameter sets the (sub)domain that the cookie is available to. \n6.The sixth and seventh parameters are set to 1, i.e. true, so the cookie is only \navailable over secure connections and it is only available over HTTP.\n<?php\n   $val = \"w3resource tutorials\";\n   setcookie(\"w3resource\", $val, time()+3600, \"/home/your_name/\", \"example.com\", 1, 1);\n   if (isset($_COOKIE['cookie']))   echo $_COOKIE[\"w3resource\"];\n?>\n\nExample: all cookies\n15\nIf you want to recover all cookies, you can use the following command\n<?php\n   print_r($_COOKIE);\n?>","storage_path":"uploads\\1774292543731_PHP_Chapter4_Eng_Cookies.pdf","summary":"Here's a detailed summary of the provided text about PHP Cookies:\n\n### PHP Cookies: Definition, Creation, and Management\n\n**1. Definition of a Cookie**\n*   **Purpose:** Primarily used to identify a user and \"remember\" information about a visitor across multiple requests or sessions.\n*   **Nature:** A small text file that the server embeds on the user's computer.\n*   **Mechanism:** Each time the same computer requests a page with a browser, it sends the relevant cookie(s) along with the request.\n*   **PHP Interaction:** PHP allows both the creation and retrieval of cookie values.\n*   **Example Use:** Storing a visitor's nickname (Login, Password, etc.) to recognize them on subsequent visits.\n\n**2. Creating Cookies with PHP (`setcookie()` function)**\n*   **Function:** Cookies are created using the `setcookie()` function.\n*   **Syntax:**\n    ```php\n    setcookie(name, value, expire, path, domain, secure, httponly);\n    ```\n*   **Required Parameter:**\n    *   `name`: The name of the cookie (e.g., \"user\", \"counter\"). This is the *only* mandatory parameter.\n*   **Optional Parameters:** All other parameters are optional.\n\n**3. Important Notes on `setcookie()` Usage**\n*   **Placement:** `setcookie()` must be called **before any `<HTML>` or `<HEAD>` tags** because cookies are sent as part of the HTTP headers.\n*   **Accessibility Timing:**\n    *   A newly set cookie will only be accessible upon the **next page load** or the **reloading of the current page**.\n    *   When the browser requests a page via HTTP, it looks for valid cookies and sends them in the HTTP request header.\n*   **`$_COOKIE` Superglobal Variable:**\n    *   `$_COOKIE` is a superglobal array that contains all cookies transmitted during the current HTTP request.\n    *   You can access a cookie's value using `$_COOKIE['cookie_name']`.\n    *   **Warning:** `$_COOKIE` is *not* immediately updated with a new cookie value within the same script execution where `setcookie()` is called. The new value will only be available in `$_COOKIE` on the *subsequent* request.\n\n**4. Detailed `setcookie()` Parameters**\n*   **`name` (Mandatory):** The name of the cookie.\n*   **`value`:** The value to store in the cookie.\n*   **`expire` (Expiration Date):**\n    *   Specifies the Unix timestamp when the cookie should expire (in seconds).\n    *   Calculated as `time() + [number_of_seconds_from_now]`.\n    *   **Persistent Cookie:** Setting a future date makes the cookie persistent.\n    *   **Session Cookie:** If omitted or set to `0`, the cookie is destroyed when the browser is closed.\n    *   **Deleting a Cookie:** Setting a past date (e.g., `time() - 3600` for one hour ago) deletes the cookie from the client's browser.\n*   **`path`:**\n    *   The path on the server where the cookie will be available.\n    *   `/`: Available on the entire domain.\n    *   `/rep/`: Available only in the `/rep/` directory and its subdirectories (e.g., `/rep/rep1/`).\n    *   Default Value: The current directory where the cookie was defined.\n*   **`domain`:**\n    *   The domain where the cookie will be available.\n    *   `.u-picardie.fr`: Indicates that all pages of sub-domains of `u-picardie.fr` can read the cookie.\n    *   Default Value: The current domain.\n*   **`secure`:**\n    *   A boolean value (0 or 1).\n    *   If `1` (true), the cookie will only be transmitted over secure HTTPS connections.\n    *   If `0` (false), it can be transmitted over HTTP or HTTPS.\n*   **`httponly`:**\n    *   A boolean value.\n    *   If `true`, the cookie is only available over the HTTP protocol and cannot be accessed by client-side scripting languages like JavaScript (enhancing security against XSS attacks).\n\n**5. Cookie Operations**\n*   **Retrieving Cookie Values:**\n    *   Use the `$_COOKIE` superglobal array: `$_COOKIE['cookie_name']`.\n    *   Example: `echo $_COOKIE[\"user\"];`\n    *   To retrieve all cookies: `print_r($_COOKIE);`\n*   **Checking if a Cookie is Set:**\n    *   Use `isset($_COOKIE['cookie_name'])`.\n    *   Example: `if (!isset($_COOKIE['user'])) { echo \"Cookie is not set!\"; }`\n*   **Modifying a Cookie:**\n    *   Simply call `setcookie()` again with the *same name* and the *new value* (and desired expiration, path, etc.).\n    *   Example: `setcookie(\"User\", \"Jaber\", time()+3600*30);`\n*   **Deleting a Cookie:**\n    *   Call `setcookie()` with the cookie's `name` and an `expiration date in the past`.\n    *   Example: `setcookie(\"user\", \"\", time() - 3600);` (value can be empty).\n*   **Checking if Cookies are Enabled:**\n    *   Set a test cookie using `setcookie()`.\n    *   On the *next* request, check if the `$_COOKIE` array has any elements using `count($_COOKIE)`. If `count($_COOKIE) > 0`, cookies are likely enabled.\n\n**6. Examples and Use Cases**\n*   **User Identification:** Storing a username or ID.\n*   **Page Visit Counter:** Incrementing a `counter` cookie on each page load.\n    *   A new session (counter reset to 1) occurs if the browser is closed and restarted (for session cookies or when `setcookie` is called without `isset` check).\n*   **Specific Domain/Path Control:** Using `path` and `domain` parameters to restrict cookie availability.\n*   **Secure Cookies:** Ensuring cookies are only sent over HTTPS using the `secure` parameter.\n*   **HTTP-Only Cookies:** Preventing JavaScript access to sensitive cookies using the `httponly` parameter.","timestamp":"2026-03-23 21:02:38"}
